\title{Stratification of opers with apparent singularities  \\
and isomonodromic bubbling}
\author{\dqd, Troy Figiel, Jörg Teschner}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
On a compact Riemann surface of genus $g \geq 2$, we study Simpson's  stratification of the de Rham moduli space of rank-2 flat connections.
Upon representing flat $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{C})$-connections by branched projective structures, or equivalently by opers with apparent singularities, the stratum containing a flat connection is determined by the minimal number of apparent singularities needed to represent it.
We construct explicit holomorphic families in which two simple apparent singularities collide and disappear, so that the family crosses into a smaller stratum; this realizes Simpson's closure relation between strata near generic points.
The analogous families of Higgs bundles are described by Baker--Akhiezer divisors on the spectral curve in which two points converge to a pair of points exchanged by the spectral involution.
We show that, before the collision, the two apparent singularities are joined by twin paths bounding a bubble, so that these families arise from the isomonodromic bubbling surgery discussed by Calsamiglia, Deroin and Francaviglia.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}


Let $C$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $g \geq 2$.
The Hitchin moduli spaces of Higgs bundles $(E, \phi)$ and the de Rham moduli spaces of flat connections $(E, \nabla)$ on $C$ 
have a natural stratification, 
defined by the Bia\l ynicki-Birula stratification on the Hodge moduli space of $\lambda$-connections.
The main goal of this paper is to explicitly describe families of Higgs bundles and flat connections, of rank 2 and with fixed $\det(E)$, 
that cross into smaller strata.
Our method is to parametrize Higgs bundles and flat connections by divisors 
on $C$ together with auxiliary parameters, 
namely lifts to spectral curves $\widetilde{C}$ and accessory parameters of apparent singularities, respectively.
The degeneration families then correspond to colliding simple points of the divisors
and have these points annihilate at the limit.

In the following, we introduce the setting in \Cref{subsec-intro-strata}, 
state the main results in \Cref{subsec-intro-results} 
and discuss relations to other subjects in \Cref{subsec-intro-discussion}.

\subsection{Natural stratification}\label{subsec-intro-strata}
\paragraph{Hodge bundles and strata}
Consider the Hodge moduli space $\mathcal{M}_{Hod}$ of rank-2 $\lambda$-connections $(\lambda, E, \nabla_\lambda)$ on $C$ with trivial determinant.
(For Higgs bundles alone, we also allow a fixed determinant $\Lambda$ of degree $1$; see \Cref{sect-preliminaries}.)
Its fibers over $\lambda = 0$ and $\lambda = 1$ are the Hitchin moduli space $\mathcal{M}_H$ of Higgs bundles and the de Rham moduli space $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ of flat $\SL_2(\C)$-connections.
The $\C^\ast$-action, defined by 
$t.(\lambda, E, \nabla_\lambda) = (t\lambda, E, t\nabla_\lambda)$, 
has fixed points contained in the nilpotent cone of the Hitchin moduli space. 
We call such fixed points \textit{Hodge bundles}.
For all $x \in \mathcal{M}_{Hod}$, the limit $\underset{t \rightarrow 0}{\lim} t.x$ is a Hodge bundle.
The attractors associated to components of Hodge bundles define the Bia\l ynicki-Birula stratification of $\mathcal{M}_{Hod}$, 
the restriction of which to $\mathcal{M}_H$ and $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ defines respectively the Bia\l ynicki-Birula stratification and Simpson's partial oper stratification on these moduli spaces \cite{simpson-iterated, collier-wentworth}.

In rank 2, the stratifications of $\mathcal{M}_H$ and $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ 
are both governed by the maximal subbundles of the underlying bundles $E$, i.e. line subbundles of maximal degree.
Specifically, the largest open stratum consists of $(E, \phi)$ or $(E, \nabla)$ with $E$ stable,
and the strata with unstable underlying bundles are labelled by the degree of the maximal subbundle.
One can further refine the open stratum by the Segre stratification of the moduli space $\mathcal{N}$ of stable bundles.

A Higgs field $\phi$ (respectively, a holomorphic connection $\nabla$) on $E$ together with a line subbundle $L \hookrightarrow E$
induces a section of $KL^{-2}\det(E)$, 
which we denote by $s_L(\phi)$ (respectively, $s_L(\nabla)$).
When this section is nonzero, we denote its divisor of zeros by $D_L(\phi)$ (respectively, $D_L(\nabla)$).
In this paper, with $\Lambda = \det(E)$, we will label the strata by
\[ d = \deg(KL^{-2}\det(E)) = 2g-2-2\deg L + \deg(\Lambda), \qquad L \hookrightarrow E \text{ maximal}. \]
Note that $d \equiv \deg(\Lambda) \pmod 2$.
The strata with $d < 2g-2$ consist of Higgs bundles and flat connections with unstable underlying bundles. 
Simpson showed that, for $d < 2g-2$, the stratum of $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ labelled by $d-2$ lies in the closure of the one labelled by $d$ \cite[Theorem~7.6]{simpson-iterated}.

\paragraph{Flat connections as branched projective structures.}
A rank-2 flat connection can be represented by a branched projective structure subordinate to $C$, or equivalently by an oper with apparent singularities (\Cref{subsec-projective-structures}).
A \emph{branched projective structure} is an atlas of local holomorphic maps from $C$ to $\mathbb{P}^1$ patched by M\"obius transformations. 
Its \emph{apparent singularities}, or branch points, are the critical points of these local maps,
and its \textit{developing map} from the universal cover of $C$ to $\mathbb{P}^1$ is defined by 
analytic continuation of one such local map.
An \emph{oper with apparent singularities} is an atlas of compatible local Schr\"odinger equations $(\partial_z^2 + q(z)) f = 0$, where $q$ has poles of order $2$ at the apparent singularities, with Laurent tails ensuring that the ratio of two solutions is holomorphic there.
For example, at a simple apparent singularity $x$,
\[ q(z) = -\frac{3}{4(z-z(x))^2} + \frac{\nu}{z-z(x)} + q_0 + \mathcal{O}(z - z(x)), \qquad \nu^2 + q_0 = 0, \]
and $\nu$ is called the \emph{accessory parameter} of $x$.
The two descriptions are related by taking the ratio $w = f_1/f_2$ of two independent local solutions.
This ratio is a local holomorphic map to $\mathbb{P}^1$ whose critical points are the apparent singularities, and a change of basis of solutions changes it by a M\"obius transformation; hence these local maps form the atlas of a branched projective structure.
Conversely, the oper is recovered from the atlas of the branched projective structure by taking Schwarzian derivatives of the local maps, $q = \frac{1}{2}\{w, z\}$.

A flat connection $(E, \nabla)$ together with a line subbundle $L \hookrightarrow E$ defines such a branched projective structure: parallel transport of the lines $L_x \hookrightarrow E_x$ gives local maps to $\mathbb{P}^1$ whose critical points, i.e.\ the apparent singularities, are the $2g-2-2\deg L$ points of $D_L(\nabla)$.
Hence the stratum of $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ labelled by $d$ consists of the flat connections for which the minimal number of apparent singularities needed to represent them is $d$ (\Cref{par-PSL2-strata}).

\subsection{Main results}\label{subsec-intro-results}
We now state the main results: how colliding simple apparent singularities define a family that crosses to a smaller stratum. 
Fix a holomorphic projective structure on $C$, e.g.\ the uniformizing one. 
For $x_0 \in C$, let $(U, z)$ be a local coordinated neighborhood centred at $x_0$ given by a chart of this projective structure, 
and for $u \in z(U)$ let $x_\pm(u)$ be the points with $z(x_\pm(u)) = \pm u$.

\paragraph{Crossing to smaller partial oper strata}
\begin{mainprop}[Reduction of apparent singularities] \label{prop-A}
	Let $\mathcal{D}$ be an oper on $C$ 
    with $d-2$ simple apparent singularities $x_3, \dots, x_d$, where $d \leq 3g-3$, and let $x_0 \in C$ be generic.
	Then there exists a holomorphic family $\{\mathcal{D}_u\}_{u\in z(U)}$ with $\mathcal{D}_0 = \mathcal{D}$ such that, for $u \neq 0$, $\mathcal{D}_u$ has simple apparent 
    singularities at $x_+(u), x_-(u), x_3, \dots, x_d$, and the accessory parameters of $x_\pm(u)$ with respect to the projective chart $z$ are of the form
	\[ \nu_\pm(u) = \pm \frac{3}{4u} + \mathcal{O}(u). \]
	(See \Cref{prop-bb-limit-3}; the precise form of $\nu_\pm(u)$ is \eqref{eqn-form-B}.)
\end{mainprop}

\begin{mainthm}[Crossing to smaller strata]\label{thm-B}
	Suppose moreover that $d$ is even and that the projective monodromy of $\mathcal{D}$ is irreducible.
	Then the holomorphic family $\{\mathcal{D}_u\}$ of \Cref{prop-A} is defined by a holomorphic family $\{[E_u, \nabla_u]\}_u$ in $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ 
	together with line subbundles $L_u \hookrightarrow E_u$ of degree
	\[ \deg L_u = g-1-\frac{d}{2} \quad \text{for } u \neq 0, \qquad \qquad \deg L_0 = g - \frac{d}{2}, \]
	and $D_{L_u}(\nabla_u) = x_+(u) + x_-(u) + x_3 + \dots + x_d$ for $u \neq 0$, $D_{L_0}(\nabla_0) = x_3 + \dots + x_d$.
	For $d < 2g-2$, $[E_u, \nabla_u]$ lies in the partial oper stratum labelled by $d$ for $u \neq 0$, 
	and in the partial oper stratum labelled by $d-2$ at $u = 0$.
	(See \Cref{prop-family-dR,cor-crossing-strata}.)
\end{mainthm}

We observe that \Cref{thm-B} gives explicit families realizing Simpson's closure relation near generic points of the smaller stratum.
On $\mathbb{P}^1$, a collision of two apparent singularities at a jump of the type of the underlying bundle was analyzed by Komyo and Saito for rank-2 parabolic connections with five regular singular points \cite[Theorem~5.5]{KomyoSaito19}; there too, the dual parameters of the colliding apparent singularities diverge.

\paragraph{Analogues for Higgs bundles}
\Cref{prop-A,thm-B} have exact analogues for Higgs bundles, in terms of Baker--Akhiezer divisors (\Cref{sect-degeneration-BA}).
If $(E, \phi)$ has a smooth spectral curve $\pi \colon \widetilde{C} \rightarrow C$, the points of $D_L(\phi)$ lift to the \emph{Baker--Akhiezer divisor} of $(L \hookrightarrow E, \phi)$ on $\widetilde{C}$ \cite{DT23}, which selects over each point one of the two sheets, i.e.\ one of the two eigenvalues of $\phi$ (\Cref{subsec-BA-divisors}).
In the analogue of \Cref{prop-A}, two points of the Baker--Akhiezer divisor on different sheets collide over $x_0$ and disappear; for $d < 2g-2$, 
the family crosses from the stratum labelled by $d$ to the one labelled by $d-2$ (\Cref{prop-Hitchin-bb}).
If the two points lie on the same sheet, they merge into a double point instead (\Cref{prop-Hitchin-db}); 
on the de Rham side, this corresponds to accessory parameters $\nu_\pm(u) = \mp \frac{1}{4u} + \mathcal{O}(1)$, which produce a double apparent singularity (\Cref{prop-double-as}).
For $d < 2g-2$, the limiting Hodge bundle of a family with a double point has a non-reduced divisor and is therefore \emph{wobbly}: its upward flow meets the nilpotent cone in more than one point \cite{HH21}.

It is clear that analogous results in higher rank hold for specific cases: 
one might degenerate Baker--Akhiezer divisors to have a summand of the form $\pi^\ast (p)$ for $p \in C$ to cross to a lower stratum.
We refer to \cite{HH21, DDX26} for the necessary background on Baker--Akhiezer divisors for higher rank cases. 

\paragraph{Relation to isomonodromic bubbling}
Bubbling is an isomonodromic surgery on branched projective structures \cite{CalsamigliaDeroinFrancaviglia2014}.
The input of the surgery is one branched projective structure and an embedded arc along which the developing map is injective.
We cut the surface along this arc 
and glue in a copy of $\mathbb{P}^1$ cut along the developed image of the arc.
The output is another branched projective structure with the same monodromy and two more apparent singularities at the end points of the arc, and is in general subordinate to a different complex structure.
The glued-in disc, a \emph{bubble}, is bounded by two \emph{twin paths}: paths between the two new branch points with the same developed image.

\begin{mainthm}[Bubbling]\label{thm-C}
	For sufficiently small $u \neq 0$, 
    the branched projective structure corresponding to $\mathcal{D}_u$ of \Cref{prop-A} 
    is the output of a bubbling surgery 
    that creates branch points that correspond to the apparent singularities $x_+(u)$ and $x_-(u)$.
    Specifically, this bubbling surgery takes as an input the branched projective structure $\sigma_u$, 
    which is obtained by removing the bubble bounded by the twin paths and identifying these twin paths, 
    and has simple apparent singularities at $x_3, \dots, x_d$.
	(See \Cref{thm-bubble}.)
\end{mainthm}

