\paragraph{Beilinson--Drinfeld approach to the geometric Langlands correspondence}
The geometric Langlands correspondence (GLC), recently proved by Gaitsgory--Raskin et al.~\cite{GLC},
was pioneered by Drinfeld \cite{Dr83} and Beilinson--Drinfeld \cite{BD}.
The special case Beilinson--Drinfeld proved belongs to that of holomorphic opers (i.e. those \textit{without} apparent singularities):
given a complex semisimple group $G$ and a holomorphic oper $\sigma$ on $C$ with gauge group being the Langlands dual $\check{G}$,
using techniques from conformal field theories they constructed the corresponding Hecke eigensheaf $\mathcal{F}_\sigma$ on the moduli stack $\mathrm{Bun}_G$ of $G$-bundles on $C$ \cite{BD}, see also \cite{Fr07}.
In particular, $\mathcal{F}_\sigma$ can be realized by a holonomic system of eigenvalue equations of differential operators called \textit{quantum Hitchin Hamiltonians} that act on $K^{1/2}_{\mathrm{Bun}_G}$.

From this point of view, one might be motivated to understand the construction of the Hecke eigensheaves $\mathcal{F}_\sigma$ 
for a general local system $\sigma$ in the proximity of holomorphic opers.
Frenkel and Teleman extended the equivalence of Beilinson and Drinfeld on the locus of opers to the formal neighborhood of this locus \cite{FT13}. 
In contrast, for $\check{G} = \mathrm{PSL}_2(\C)$ and odd $g$, our results suggest a concrete description of an analytic neighborhood of the locus of holomorphic opers.
Namely, we expect that a generic flat connection in a neighborhood of opers can be represented 
by an oper with $3g-3$ apparent singularities, 
forming $\frac{3g-3}{2}$ pairs of nearby points, each joined by twin paths bounding a small bubble.
Debubbling all of these bubbles gives a holomorphic oper with the same monodromy on a nearby Riemann surface.

