\subsection{Spectral correspondence and integrable structure}\label{subsec-spectral}
The Hitchin fibration $h: \mathcal{M}_H \rightarrow H^0(K^2)$ associates to the isomorphism class of a Higgs bundle $(E, \phi)$ the quadratic differential $q = \det(\phi)$.
A generic Hitchin fiber is isomorphic to an abelian variety, namely the Prym variety of the associated spectral curve.
This endows $\mathcal{M}_H$ with the structure of an algebraic integrable system \cite{Hit87b}.

\paragraph{Spectral curves.}
We recall that associated to a quadratic differential $q = \det(\phi)$
is a spectral curve $\widetilde{C}_{q}$ embedded in the total space of $T^\ast C$.
The spectral curve encodes the eigen-values of the Higgs field:
concretely, if $z$ is the coordinate of an open set $U \subset C$,
$v$ the fiber coordinate of the restriction of $T^\ast C$ to $U$
and $\phi (z) = \begin{pmatrix} a(z) & b(z) \\ c(z) & - a(z) \end{pmatrix}$ locally,
then locally $\widetilde{C}_q$ is defined by
\begin{align}
	v^2 + q(z) = v^2  - a(z)^2 - b(z) c(z) = 0.
	\label{spectral-curve-eqn}
\end{align}
Here $v$ is the tautological section of $T^\ast C$, and $v^2 + q$ is the characteristic polynomial of $\phi$.
The morphism $\widetilde{C}_q \overset{\pi}{\rightarrow} C$ induced by $T^\ast C \rightarrow C$ is a $2:1$ covering that branches at the zeroes of $\det(\phi)$.
The involution $\sigma$ of $\widetilde{C}_q$ interchanges points
corresponding to the eigenvalues $v = \pm (-q(z))^{1/2}$ of $\phi(z)$.
We say a quadratic differential $q$ and its associated spectral curve $\widetilde{C}_q$ are non-degenerate if the zeroes of $q$ are all simple.
In this case, $\widetilde{C}_q$ is a smooth compact Riemann surface of genus $\tilde{g} = 4g-3$,
and in particular $\pi^\ast(K)$ has a canonical section defined by $v$ that vanishes precisely at the ramification divisor $R_q$ of $\widetilde{C}_q$.
A spectral curve $\widetilde{C}_q$ is called degenerate when $q$ has zeroes of non-trivial multiplicity;
the most degenerate case is $q=0$, and we call the fiber $h^{-1}(0)$ the nilpotent cone.

\paragraph{Spectral line bundles.}
Let $(E, \phi) \in \mathcal{M}_H(\Lambda)$ be a Higgs bundle with
non-degenerate spectral curve $\widetilde{C}_q \overset{\pi}{\rightarrow} C$.
The cokernel of the map
$ \pi^\ast(E K^{-1}) \overset{\pi^\ast(\phi) - v}{\longrightarrow} \pi^\ast(E) $
is a line bundle $\mathcal{L}_{(E,\phi)}$ on $\widetilde{C}_q$
which we will call the spectral line bundle of $(E,\phi)$.
{%
At a point $\tilde{p} = (z, v)$ of $\widetilde{C}_q$ which is not a ramification point, the image of $\pi^\ast(\phi) - v$ is the eigen-line of $\phi(z)$ with eigen-value $-v$, so the fiber of $\mathcal{L}_{(E,\phi)}$ at $\tilde{p}$ is the quotient of $E_z$ by this eigen-line.
}%
The spectral correspondence \cite{Hit87a, BNR}
is a correspondence between Higgs bundles and their spectral line bundles,
namely that the underlying bundle can be recovered by the direct image
$$ E \simeq \pi_\ast \mathcal{L}_{(E, \phi)},
\qquad \qquad \deg \left( \mathcal{L}_{(E,\phi)} \right) = 2g-2 +\deg(\Lambda), \qquad \qquad \phi = \pi_\ast v. $$
It follows that the fiber $h^{-1}(q)$ of the Hitchin fibration
$h: \mathcal{M}_H(\Lambda) \rightarrow H^0(C, K^2)$ over a non-degenerate $q$
is canonically isomorphic to
\begin{equation}\label{L-L-sigma}
	\mathrm{Prym}^{2g - 2 +\deg(\Lambda)}(\widetilde{C}_q, \Lambda) \coloneqq \left\{ \mathcal{L} \in \mathrm{Pic}^{2g - 2 +\deg(\Lambda)}(\widetilde{C}_q)
	\ \middle| \ \mathcal{L} \otimes \sigma^\ast\mathcal{L} \simeq \pi^\ast(K \Lambda) \right\},
\end{equation}
which is a torsor over the Prym variety of $\widetilde{C}_q \overset{\pi}{\rightarrow} C$.

\section{Baker--Akhiezer divisors of Higgs bundles and apparent singularities of projective connections}\label{sect-BA-apparent}

\subsection{Baker--Akhiezer divisors}\label{subsec-BA-divisors}
In this subsection we recall the notion of Baker--Akhiezer divisors on non-degenerate spectral curves.

The input data to define these divisors
consist of a Higgs bundle $(E,\phi)$ with non-degenerate associated spectral curve and an injection of a line bundle $L$ to $E$.
While the terminology for these divisors is recent \cite{DT23} and inspired by the literature on integrable systems,
the usage of these divisors is not entirely new.
Hitchin in his original paper \cite{Hit87a} already characterized Higgs bundles with underlying unstable bundles in terms of these divisors,
and the more recent work \cite{HH21} of Hausel-Hitchin on very stable Higgs bundles $\alpha$ and Fourier--Mukai transforms of $\alpha^+$
also made extensive use of related divisors.


\subsubsection{Definitions and basic properties}
Let $(E, \phi) \in \mathcal{M}_H(\Lambda)$ be a Higgs bundle with non-degenerate spectral curve $\widetilde{C} \overset{\pi}{\rightarrow} C$.
Consider an injection $L \overset{i}{\rightarrow} E$ from a line bundle $L$ into $E$,
which a priori is not necessarily an embedding and can vanish at some point on $C$.
We will use the notation $L \overset{i}{\hookrightarrow} E$ 
for an injection which has no zero, i.e. an embedding that makes $L$ into a subbundle of $E$.

\begin{definition}\label{def-BA-divisor-formal}
	The \textit{Baker--Akhiezer divisor}%
	\footnote{In local frames adapted to $\pi^\ast L$, the line bundle $\pi^\ast(K^{-1}) \mathcal{L}_{(E,\phi)}$ of eigenvectors of $\pi^\ast(\phi)$ has sections of the form $\left(\begin{smallmatrix} v + a(z) \\ c(z) \end{smallmatrix} \right)$,
	reminiscent of the Baker--Akhiezer function in the theory of integrable systems \cite{DT23}, \cite[Remark~2.3]{Dinh24wobbly}.}
	$\widetilde{D}_i(\phi)$ of the data $(L \overset{i}{\rightarrow} E, \phi)$ is the zero divisor of the morphism of line bundles on $\widetilde{C}$
	\begin{equation} \label{BA-div-def-composition}
		\pi^\ast(L) \overset{\pi^\ast i}{\longrightarrow} \pi^\ast(E) \longrightarrow \mathcal{L}_{(E, \phi)}.
	\end{equation}
\end{definition}
We note that this definition of Baker--Akhiezer divisors carries straightforwardly to higher rank cases \cite{HH21, DDX26}.

If $L \overset{i}{\rightarrow} E$ vanishes at $p \in C$, then $\pi^\ast(p) < \widetilde{D}_i(\phi)$ (this holds even if $p$ is a branch point).
