\section{Introduction}

In this paper we introduce a new method for solving nonlinear Schrödinger equations which is based on the trilinear restriction estimate. The general analysis of nonlinear dispersive PDEs relies heavily on linear estimates (i.e. Strichartz estimates) and bilinear estimates, in particular the bilinear $L^2$ estimate. These estimates are based on transferring estimates known for free waves, for example linear and multilinear restriction estimates, to more general functions in a resolution space where solutions to the underlying nonlinear PDE can be constructed. Transference of linear Strichartz estimates is by now standard. Transference of the bilinear $L^2$ estimate has been made possible by the development of delicate resolution structures such as the $X^{s,b}$ spaces, the $U^2,V^2$ structures adapted to the underlying dispersive flow, or using adapted frames. A more complete exposition of these spaces and applications is provided in Section \ref{s:GA}. 

The first step for a transference theory for trilinear restriction estimates is to establish the estimate for free waves. Recent developments in harmonic analysis, particularly the introduction of the polynomial partitioning method in the study of the restriction conjecture by Guth \cite{Gu-I,Gu-II}, have provided an opening for understanding the trilinear and higher order multilinear restriction estimates. These arguments typically establish ``broad" versions of the restriction estimate, which are well-suited for applications to restriction theory. However, for applications to nonlinear PDEs one needs the genuine multilinear estimates. In \cite{Be-mrec}, the first author adapted these ideas to prove the $(n-1)$-linear restriction estimate in $\R^n$. 

To keep the exposition simpler and more concrete, we assume that we work in $\R^4=\R^3\times\R$, that is three spatial dimensions and one temporal dimension, and focus on the case of paraboloids/Schrödinger equations; this is also the relevant context for the application to the quadratic NLS. In this setting we have the following result.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 1.2]{Be-mrec}}] \label{TSS}
Let $f_1, f_2, f_3$ be supported in sets $\Omega_1, \Omega_2, \Omega_3\subset\R^3$ satisfying the transversality condition
\begin{equation} \label{Nres}
|N(\xi_1) \wedge N(\xi_2) \wedge N(\xi_3)| \ges 1, \quad \forall \xi_i \in \Omega_i, i=1,2,3,
\end{equation}
where $N(\xi)$ is the unit normal to the paraboloid $\tau=\xi^2$ in $\R^4$. Then for any $p>\frac{14}{15}$ the following holds true
\begin{equation} \label{TE-b}
    \norm{e^{-it\Delta} f_1 \cdot e^{-it\Delta} f_2 \cdot e^{-it\Delta} f_3}_{L^p(\R^4)}\lesssim \norm{f_1}_{L^2(\R^3)} \norm{f_2}_{L^2(\R^3)} \norm{f_3}_{L^2(\R^3)}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

In this paper we develop a transference theory which allows this trilinear estimate to be deployed in the analysis of nonlinear PDEs. In this context, what is actually needed is a trilinear estimate in $L^1(\R^4)$ but in order to obtain it one relies on the stronger estimate above in $L^p$ with $p<1$. Precisely, we prove a trilinear estimate for the low modulation components of functions in  $V^2_\Delta$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm trilinear transference}
Let $u, v, w \in V^2_\Delta$ such that $Q_{\les 1} u, Q_{\les 1} v, Q_{\les 1} w$ have localization properties similar to the ones in Theorem \ref{TSS}. Then  the following estimate holds true:
\begin{equation} \label{TV2s}
    \norm{Q_{\les 1}u \cdot Q_{\les 1}v \cdot Q_{\les 1}w}_{L^1(\R^4)}\lesssim \norm{u}_{V^2_\Delta} \norm{v}_{V^2_\Delta} \norm{w}_{V^2_\Delta}.
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

The operator $Q_{\les 1}$ localizes in modulation, that is $|\tau-\xi^2| \les 1$, and it is defined in Section \ref{subsect:not}. Thus, the theorem provides, in some sense, only a partial result since we do not obtain the full estimate for the term $\norm{ u \cdot v \cdot w}_{L^1(\R^4)}$. However, this is not a problem at all in applications, since, armed with \eqref{TV2s}, one is left with the analysis of the trilinear expression $\norm{ u \cdot v \cdot w}_{L^1(\R^4)}$ under the additional assumption that at least one term has high modulation ($\ges 1$). In this case the linear Strichartz and bilinear $L^2$ estimates become more effective. It is precisely in the interaction  $Q_{\les 1}u \cdot Q_{\les 1}v \cdot \overline{Q_{\les 1}w}$ where the resonances live, and the novelty of our method is in being able to obtain the estimate for this term. 

The space $V^2_\Delta$ is defined in section \ref{s:RS}. It is important that the trilinear term is controlled in terms of $V^2_\Delta$ norms only as this makes the estimate very effective in the construction of solutions. It is rather standard to obtain \eqref{TV2s} using the stronger norms $U^2_\Delta$ instead of $V^2_\Delta$ - see for example \cite{Ca, Ca-note, CaHe} - and we provide a proof of this in the appendix. However, this alone would not be enough in applications to PDE.

Now we come to a rather important point; for technical reasons, we actually need a more general form of the above result, see Theorem \ref{trilml}. While Theorem \ref{TV2s} contains one of the new and powerful transference techniques, it is significantly easier than what we actually need in this paper where (after rescaling arguments) it suffices to estimate the term $\norm{Q_{\les 2k}u_k \cdot Q_{\les 2k}v_0 \cdot Q_{\les 2k}w_0}_{L^1(\R^4)}$, where $u_k$ is localized at frequency $2^k \ll 1$, $v_0, w_0$ are localized at frequency $\approx 1$. The main obstacle in estimating this term is that the analogue of \eqref{Nres} in this set-up has a lower bound of $2^k \ll 1$, which represents a degenerate transversality condition for the trilinear restriction estimate. In order to deal with this, we use a standard parabolic rescaling argument to transform the frequency supports into a set-up with transversality constant $\gtrsim 1$. The price to pay is that one term, while still on the paraboloid, moves into the regime with $|\xi| \gg 1$ and here we now encounter degenerate curvature instead. While the full version of \cite[Theorem 1.2]{Be-mrec} for general hypersurfaces is rather robust in that it assumes a minimal amount of curvature information, it is actually not enough for this concrete application we have in mind, except in the case $2^k \approx 1$. In a companion paper \cite{BeCh} we augment the theory in \cite{Be-mrec} to account for even more degeneracy in the curvature information on the underlying hypersurfaces. In fact, because of these degenerate curvature assumptions, even the nature of \eqref{TE-b} changes, see Theorem \ref{ltri1}. 

As an application of this theoretical framework, we have identified the quadratic NLS in three spatial dimensions to be the simplest nonlinear model where we can effectively deploy our new theory. We consider the following quadratic NLS
\begin{equation} \label{QNLS}
(i \partial_t - \Delta) u = c_1 u^2 + c_2 \bar u^2, \quad u(0,x)=u_0(x), 
\end{equation}
where $u: \R^n \times \R \rightarrow \C$ and $c_1,c_2\in\C$. The natural scaling for this equation is $s_c=\frac{n}{2}-2$. 

\eqref{QNLS} has been studied extensively, with works covering a variety of problems such as local well-posedness, global well-posedness, scattering,and ill-posedness. See for instance \cite{bejenaru_sharp_2006, BeDa, CaWe, colliander_bilinear_2001, KeGuVe, MuToTa, NaTaTs, Tsu, tao_multilinear_2001}, and the references therein. Below we describe some of the results most relevant to our current work.

In dimensions $n \geq 4$, global well-posedness for \eqref{QNLS} in the critical space was established by Cazenave and Weissler in \cite{CaWe}. The theory is essentially based on linear Strichartz estimates. The situation changes substantially once $n=3$. Assuming initial data in $H^s(\R^3)$, there are only local (in time) well-posedness results. Local well-posedness for $s > -\frac12$ was established by Tao in \cite{tao_multilinear_2001} using a generalization of the bilinear $X^{s,b}$ estimates from \cite{colliander_bilinear_2001} to higher dimensions. The major obstacle in developing a global in time theory in Sobolev spaces is the lack of an effective way to deal with resonances which are present in this problem.  

On the other hand, for regular data with sufficient spatial decay, the corresponding free solution has temporal decay $\approx t^{-\frac32}$ which is integrable in time. This suggests that a global theory may be within reach for such initial data. 

In \cite{HaNa}, Hayashi and Naumkin established the first global well-posedness and scattering  result for \eqref{QNLS} for small data with $H^{10}$ regularity and decay of type $\la x \ra^{10} u_0 \in L^2$. In \cite{HaMiNa}, Hayashi, Mizumachi, and Naumkin further refined the result by lowering the regularity and decay requirements, as well as providing additional decay properties of the corresponding solution and, in \cite{Ka} Kawahara further refined a decay property of the solutions. All these works rely on the vector field method. 

In \cite{GeMaSh-1}, Germain, Masmoudi, and Shatah introduced the space-time resonance method precisely in the context of the quadratic NLS model in 3D. In this paper, the authors established global well-posedness and scattering for \eqref{QNLS} with initial data requiring less regularity and decay than the above mentioned works; a precise quantification of the level of regularity and decay imposed on the data is not straightforward, given that their conditions on the initial data are made in a non-standard fashion. 

To the best of our knowledge, as explained above, all the global (in time) results in 3D for \eqref{QNLS} are in the context of data that have high regularity and some spatial decay.

The second main result of this paper is a proof of global well-posedness and scattering for \eqref{QNLS} in the critical Sobolev space.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm gwp}
The equation \eqref{QNLS} with small initial data $u_0 \in \dot H^{-\frac12}(\R^3)$ is globally well-posed, scatters to free solutions, and has Lipschitz dependence on its initial data. The solution is also unique in the resolution space $\dot X^{-\frac12} \subset C_t \dot H^{-\frac12}$. 

Moreover, if the initial data is more regular, say $u_0 \in H^s(\R^3)$ for some $s>0$ and its critical norm $\|u_0\|_{\dot H^{-\frac12}(\R^3)}$ is small enough, then the global solution $u(t)$ preserves the regularity.
\end{theorem}

We remark that among the two components of the nonlinearity in \eqref{QNLS}, $(\bar u)^2$ is significantly easier, given the lack of resonances. This is why the paper focuses mostly on the $u^2$ component. The reader may also notice that we do not include the other quadratic component $|u|^2=u \cdot \bar u$ in our analysis. This nonlinearity is known to be rather difficult to handle. In \cite{tao_multilinear_2001}, Tao proved local well-posedness for initial data in $H^s(\R^3)$ when $s>-\frac{1}{4}$. In \cite{ginibre_almost_1995}, Ginibre and Hayashi proved almost global existence in weighted Sobolev spaces using the vector field method. To the best of our knowledge, the global well-posedness problem for this nonlinearity is still open, even for localized and smooth data using the space-time resonance method. In this context, we should also mention the work \cite{guo_global_2025}, where Guo, Liu, and Song proved global well-posedness and scattering for the fractional Schrödinger equation $(i\partial_t-D^\alpha)u=\abs{u}^2$ where $\alpha\in(1,2)$ using the method of space-time resonances. The main difficulty for this nonlinearity is the high-high-to-low interaction which is difficult to control - see Section \ref{s:RA} for some more details on this.

Finally, we remark that after reading this introduction, the flow of the rest of our paper may seem inverted as the quadratic NLS takes the center stage, while the trilinear restriction estimate and the transference theory may be seen as supporting results. There is a good reason for this. The result in Theorem \ref{TSS} and its transference were the starting point of this work; however when working on the quadratic NLS we discovered the need for a far more general version of \eqref{Nres}  not covered by the work of the first author in \cite{Be-mrec}. This  motivated the work in the companion paper \cite{BeCh}, by the first and third authors, which augments that theory so that it delivers the necessary tools to complete the analysis of the quadratic NLS \eqref{QNLS}. In nonlinear PDEs the practical problem at hand dictates the specific multilinear restriction estimate that is needed along with the transference; in our particular case,  even the formalization of \eqref{TE-b} needs nontrivial adjustments.

We do believe that the strategy developed here should work in other nonlinear problems where resonances are present and the classical methods based on linear and bilinear estimates are not strong enough to close the analysis. We provide a robust method of transferring trilinear restriction estimates, hence the central problem remains that of deriving the key trilinear restriction estimate tailored to the particular setup considered. We think that the work in \cite{Be-mrec} and \cite{BeCh} provide enough guidance on how this can be approached, if not already covered by the theory in those papers. 

