\section{Outline of the strategy}\label{s:GA}


In this section, we provide a more complete outline of how our new method fits within the context of the general perturbative analysis of dispersive PDEs, in particular the current iteration techniques based on the linear and bilinear restriction theory.

The standard road map to iterate a semilinear dispersive PDE is as follows. For concreteness, consider a general nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation:
\[
(i \partial_t - \Delta)u = N(u, \nabla u), \quad u(0,x)=u_{0}(x),
\]
where $u: \R \times \R^n \rightarrow \C$. One seeks to identify two spaces $X,Y$ (with $X$ being a Banach space) with the following two properties:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item Solvability of the inhomogeneous equation
    \begin{equation} \label{LXY}
        (i \partial_t - \Delta)u = f, \quad u(0,x)=u_{0}(x),
    \end{equation}
    holds true as follows
    \[
    \|u\|_{X} \les \|f\|_{Y} + \|u_0\|_{L^2(\R^n)}.
    \]
    \item Control of the nonlinearity
    \begin{equation} \label{Nuvb}
        \|N(u,\nabla u)\|_Y \les \| u \|_X^p,
    \end{equation}
    where $p$ is the ``degree" of the nonlinearity of $N(u)$; variations may appear. 
\end{enumerate}

$X$ is referred to as the resolution space for the nonlinear PDE. We ignore the regularity at which one carries out the theory in order to keep this presentation concise but we note that one typically adds regularity to $X$ either directly or by defining spaces $X_k$ meant to measure solutions localized at frequency $\approx 2^k$ and then summing the $X_k$ information (similar to how Sobolev regularity is recouped from information on the frequency localized components).

If, in addition, the resolution space satisfies $X^* \subset Y$ (in a sense that needs to be made precise) then, for the purpose of \eqref{Nuvb}, it suffices to establish instead
\begin{equation} \label{NXY}
|\la N(u, \nabla u), v \ra| = |\int N(u,\nabla u) \bar v dx dt| \les \| u \|_{X}^p \|v\|_{X}.
\end{equation}
Indeed, this guarantees that $N(u,\nabla u) \in X^* \subset Y$ with the appropriate bounds and \eqref{Nuvb} follows.

Linear and multilinear restriction estimates can provide very powerful tools for establishing estimates of the form \eqref{NXY}, especially in the translation invariant setting. For example, for the quadratic nonlinearity $N(u)=u^2$, \eqref{NXY} becomes
\begin{equation} \label{NXYu2}
|\int u^2 \bar v dx dt| \les \| u \|_{X}^2 \|v\|_{X}.
\end{equation}
From this estimate, the connection between \eqref{QNLS} and the trilinear restriction estimate may start to become clearer. In order to formalize these ideas, we start by recalling the linear and bilinear theory for general dispersive flows before discussing the trilinear restriction estimate in this context.

\subsection{Linear and bilinear restriction theory}

Linear restriction estimates, i.e. Strichartz estimates, play a fundamental role in the analysis of dispersive PDEs. For a comprehensive introduction to the topic of Strichartz estimates and their applications, the books of Cazenave \cite{Caz-book} and Tao \cite{Tao-book} are very good references.

In general, Strichartz estimates provide a very effective way of quantifying the dispersive properties of waves, especially in the translation invariant setting. For example, as described in the introduction, in dimensions $n\geq 4$, linear Strichartz estimates are strong enough to provide a perturbative theory for \eqref{QNLS} able to obtain global solutions for small data.

However, there are important nonlinear models where the Strichartz estimates do not capture all the phenomenology necessary to provide a satisfactory perturbative theory even in the case of small initial data; this is the case when the relative strength of the nonlinearity increases. One of the limitations of the Strichartz estimates comes from the fact that they quantify the dispersive properties of only a single wave.

A major improvement in the analysis comes from studying the interaction of multiple waves. Let us recall the bilinear $L^2_{t,x}$ estimate for the Schr\"odinger equation,
\begin{equation} \label{bilS}
\|e^{-it\Delta} f \cdot e^{-it\Delta} g  \|_{L^2_{t,x}} \les 2^{\frac{(n-1)k_1-k_2}2} \|f\|_{L^2(\R^n)} \|g\|_{L^2(\R^n)},
\end{equation}
where $f$ is localized at frequency $\approx 2^{k_1}$, $g$ is localized at frequency $\approx 2^{k_2}$ and $k_1 \leq k_2$. If $n=1$, \eqref{bilS} requires additional transversality assumptions when $|k_1-k_2| \les 1$, but it holds as stated if $n \geq 2$. In this paper we work in dimensions $n \geq 2$ and all the statements made from here on are meant for $n \geq 2$. 

A similar estimate holds true for the wave equation
\begin{equation} \label{bilW}
\|e^{it|\nabla|} f \cdot e^{it|\nabla|} g  \|_{L^2_{t,x}(\R^{n+1})} \les 2^{\frac{(n-1)k_1}2} \theta^{\frac{n-3}2} \|f\|_{L^2(\R^n)} \|g\|_{L^2(\R^n)},
\end{equation}
where $f,g$ are localized at frequency $2^{k_1}$, respectively $2^{k_2}$ and the angle between their support is $\approx \theta$. 

Similar estimates to \eqref{bilS} and \eqref{bilW} hold for other linear dispersive flows too. For all these estimates, the key idea is that the Fourier transform of any free solution to a dispersive equation with constant coefficients ``lives" on the characteristic surface of the equation. For example, free solutions to the Schr\"odinger equation live on the paraboloid $\{\tau-\abs{\xi}^2=0\}$, free solutions to the wave equation live on the cone $\{\tau\pm\abs{\xi}=0\}$, etc. Formally, for the Schrödinger equation for example, one can write $e^{-it\Delta} f = \int e^{i (x,t) \cdot (\xi,\tau)} \hat f(\xi) \delta_{\phi(\xi,\tau)=0} d \xi d \tau$ where $\phi(\xi,\tau)=\tau-\xi^2$. By studying the bilinear $L^2$ estimates on the Fourier side (through Plancherel), one can use the geometry of these surfaces to understand the spacetime interactions of the waves. We provide an overview of these arguments here but note that they are quite standard and can be found in many places, see for instance Chapter 5 of \cite{KTV}.

Let $\Sigma$ be a hypersurface in $\R^d$ which is a non-degenerated level set of the functions $\phi$. A natural way to define $\delta_{\phi=0}$ is given by
\[
\delta_{\phi=0} = |\nabla \phi|^{-1} d \mathcal{H}^{d-1} |_{\phi=0},
\]
where $\mathcal{H}^{d-1}$ is the $d-1$ Hausdorff measure on $\Sigma$.  We now consider two such hypersurfaces $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$. For each $(x,y) \in \Sigma_1 \times \Sigma_2$ we let $\Sigma(x,y)=\{y+\Sigma_1\} \cap (x+\Sigma_2)$ and let $L(x,y)$ be the square root of 
\[
\int_{\Sigma(x,y)} [ |\nabla \phi_1(z-y)|^2 |\nabla \phi_2(z-x)|^2 - \la \nabla \phi_1(z-y), \nabla \phi_2(z-x) \ra^2  ]^{-\frac12} d \mathcal{H}^{d-2};
\]
Then we let
\[
L=\sup_{x \in \Sigma_1, y \in \Sigma_2} L(x,y).
\]
With the above notation, the following holds true, see Theorem 5.1 in \cite{KTV},
\begin{equation} \label{bilG}
\| f_1 \delta_{\phi_1=0} \ast f_2 \delta_{\phi_2=0} \|_{L^2(\R^d)} 
\leq L \| f_1 |\nabla \phi_1|^{-1} \|_{L^2(\Sigma_1)} \| f_2 |\nabla \phi_2|^{-1} \|_{L^2(\Sigma_2)}.
\end{equation}
The relation between \eqref{bilG} and \eqref{bilS}/\eqref{bilW} is formalized via Plancherel: instead of estimating a product in $L^2_{t,x}$ as stated in \eqref{bilS}/\eqref{bilW}, one estimates its Fourier transform (which is a convolution) in $L^2_{\tau,\xi}$. It is an easy exercise to verify that \eqref{bilS} and \eqref{bilW} are particular cases of \eqref{bilG}. 

Let us unpack the quantities that appear above. To keep things simpler we assume that, in the support of $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$ we have $\|\nabla \phi_1\|_{L^\infty} \approx 1, \|\nabla \phi_2\|_{L^\infty} \approx 1$; this setup can be obtained via rescaling arguments in general. In this case 
\[
[ |\nabla \phi_1(z-y)|^2 |\nabla \phi_2(z-x)|^2 - \la \nabla \phi_1(z-y), \nabla \phi_2(z-x) \ra^2  ]^{-\frac12} \ges \theta^{-1},
\]
where 
\[
\theta = \inf_{x \in \Sigma_1, y \in \Sigma_2} \angle(N_x \Sigma_1, N_y \Sigma_2),
\]
and $N_x \Sigma_1$ is the normal to $\Sigma_1$ at $x \in \Sigma_1$, and similarly for $N_y \Sigma_2$. Thus, under this setup, \eqref{bilG} can be simplified to
\begin{equation} \label{bilGe}
\| f_1 \delta_{\phi_1=0} \ast f_2 \delta_{\phi_2=0} \|_{L^2(\R^d)} 
\leq \theta^{-\frac12} [\sup_{x \in \Sigma_1, y \in \Sigma_2} \mu_{\mathcal{H}^{d-2}} (\Sigma(x,y))]^\frac12 \| f_1 \|_{L^2(\Sigma_1)} \| f_2 \|_{L^2(\Sigma_2)}.
\end{equation}
The bilinear $L^2$ estimate picks out two important factors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The transversality between $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$, quantified by the minimum angle between normals from each surface; the estimate degenerates as $\theta \rightarrow 0$ via a factor of $\theta^{-\frac12}$

    \item The maximum size (quantified by the $\mathcal{H}^{d-2}$ measure) of the intersection between any two translates of $\Sigma_1$ and $\Sigma_2$
\end{itemize}
The above interpretation can be done without assuming the normalizations $\|\nabla \phi_1\|_{L^\infty} \approx 1$ and $\|\nabla \phi_2\|_{L^\infty} \approx 1$, but then the translation would be obscured by the additional factors of $\|\nabla \phi_1\|_{L^\infty(\Sigma_1)}, \|\nabla \phi_2\|_{L^\infty(\Sigma_2)}$. 

We recall that these bilinear estimates are for free waves, that is for solutions to the underlying linear PDE. In the analysis of nonlinear PDEs, one needs to work with more general objects (than free waves). In particular, to prove estimates of the form \eqref{NXY}, one needs to transfer these free wave estimates to more general resolution spaces - this is why designing effective resolution spaces in which solutions to the nonlinear PDE can be constructed and can also replicate the corresponding bilinear $L^2_{t,x}$ estimate has been a very important topic. In \cite{Be-rs} the first author has reviewed the relevant literature of the key resolution structures used for this purpose; we summarize it here and refer the reader to Section 1 of \cite{Be-rs} for additional details.    

\begin{itemize}

\item \bf The $X^{s,b}$ spaces. \rm These spaces appeared in the works of Beals \cite{Bea} (for the wave equation), Bourgain \cite{Bo-1, Bo-2} (for the Schr\"odinger and KdV equations) and Klainerman and Machedon \cite{KlMa} (for the wave equation) and were the first structures known to be effective in replicating estimates known for bilinear interactions of free waves.

Since the bilinear estimate is based in $L^2_{t,x}$, one can use Plancherel and run explicit computations to achieve the transference.

\item \bf The $U^2, V^2$ spaces adapted to the flow. \rm These spaces were introduced by Tataru in the context of the wave equation and later adapted to other flows, see Tataru and Koch \cite{KT, KT1,KT2},  Bejenaru and Tataru \cite{BeTa-2}, Hadac, Herr, and Koch \cite{HHK}, Herr, Tataru, and Tzvetkov \cite{HeTaTz}, to name just the very first works using them.

Based on the definition of the $U^2$ space, it is easy to lift the bilinear $L^2$ estimate to functions in $U^2$. The space $V^2$ provides access to all non-endpoint Strichartz estimates and this implies a (weaker) bilinear $L^2$ estimate. Further optimizing the interpolation process provides the bilinear $L^2$ estimate with some log-type losses for functions in $V^2$; this is necessary as one cannot use (the better) $U^2$ as a resolution space. We remark that more general bilinear $L^p_t L^q_x$ estimates in $U^2$, in particular with $p,q<2$, were proved by Candy and Herr \cite{CaHe} and Candy \cite{Ca}. They also observed that in certain cases one can use $V^2$ for one of the functions without picking up the aforementioned loss.

\item \bf The spaces based on adapted frames. \rm  In studying the wave maps equation, Tataru circumvented the lack of a standard $L^2_t L^\infty_x$ end-point Strichartz estimate by recovering such estimates in adapted frames: fairly rigid frames (and many of them) are needed to capture estimates of type $L^2_{t_\Theta} L^\infty_{x_\Theta}$, and this is paired with energy estimates of type $L^\infty_{t_\Theta} L^2_{x_\Theta}$ to recover the bilinear $L^2$ estimate in \eqref{bilW}. This approach was effective in the study of wave maps \cite{Ta-2}, and similar constructions were used for Schr\"odinger maps in \cite{BIKT-1} and Klein-Gordon type problems, see \cite{BeHe}. 

The rather intricate construction of these resolution spaces is designed so as to recover the clean bilinear $L^2$ estimate. 
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Trilinear restriction theory}

While linear and bilinear estimates are extremely powerful and have been used to study many problems, they have limitations. For example, as described in the introduction, in dimension $n=3$, even bilinear estimates are not strong enough to provide a perturbative theory for \eqref{QNLS}. The major issue is that the nonlinearity has many resonances and there is no special structure, such as a null form, to ``defeat" them in the translation invariant setup. The main goal of this paper is to establish that deploying the trilinear restriction estimate achieves precisely this objective. To the best of our knowledge, there is no result in the current literature which implements a trilinear (or higher order) version of \eqref{bilS} or \eqref{bilW}, or more generally of \eqref{bilG}.

Our exposition below focuses on the explicit problem at hand, the quadratic NLS. However the key features that makes this problem difficult (large set of resonances and lack of a special structure) appear in other nonlinear problems and the method we describe below is likely to be useful in such problems. 

Assuming for now that we have a resolution space $X$ satisfying $X^\ast\subset Y$, then in order to prove \eqref{NXY}, the first natural question to ask is whether the corresponding trilinear estimate holds true for free waves, that is
\begin{equation} \label{trifw}
|\int e^{-it\Delta} f \cdot e^{-it\Delta} g \cdot \overline{e^{-it\Delta} h} dx dt| \les \|f\|_{L^2} \|g\|_{L^2} \|h\|_{L^2}.
\end{equation}
Directly as stated, this is a simpler problem because, just as in the case of the bilinear estimates, this can be restated in the Fourier variables as follows
\[
\int e^{-it\Delta} f \cdot e^{-it\Delta} g \cdot \overline{e^{-it\Delta} h} dx dt= (\tilde f \delta_{\tau-\xi^2=0} \ast \tilde g \delta_{\tau-\xi^2=0}
\ast \tilde h \delta_{\tau+\xi^2=0})(0);
\]
here $\tilde f$ is the ``lift" of $f$ to the characteristic surface and
$\| |\nabla \phi|^{\frac12} \tilde f\|_{L^2(\Sigma)}^2 \approx  \| f\|_{L^2}$, where $\Sigma$ is the paraboloid $\tau=\xi^2$ and $L^2(\Sigma)$ is constructed using the $\mathcal{H}^3$ measure; $\tilde g$ and $\tilde h$ are constructed in a similar fashion. For simplicity we assume that $f,g,h$ are supported at frequency $\les 1$, in which case $\|\tilde f\|_{L^2} \approx \|f\|_{L^2}$ and so on. Estimates of type
\begin{equation} \label{trint}
\|\int f_1 \delta_{\Sigma_1} \ast f_2 \delta_{\Sigma_2} \ast f_3 \delta_{\Sigma_3} \|_{L^\infty} \les \theta^{-\frac12} \|f_1\|_{L^2} \|f_2\|_{L^2} \|f_3\|_{L^2}, 
\end{equation}
have previously appeared in the literature, see for instance \cite{BeCawr} and \cite{BeHeTa}. Here $\theta$ quantifies the transversality between the supports of the three surfaces, that is
\[
\theta = \inf \{|N_{\zeta_1} \Sigma_1 \wedge N_{\zeta_2} \Sigma_2 \wedge N_{\zeta_3} \Sigma_3|, \zeta_i \in \Sigma_i \}, 
\]
where $N_\zeta \Sigma$ denotes the normal to $\Sigma$ at $\zeta \in \Sigma$. Notice the similarity between \eqref{bilGe} and \eqref{trint} when it comes to the dependence on the $\theta$ quantifying the degree of transversality between the hypersurfaces involved. 

However, in applications such as \eqref{QNLS}, one needs the stronger version of \eqref{trint}
\begin{equation} \label{triL1}
\|\mathcal{E}_1 f_1  \cdot \mathcal{E}_2 f_2 \cdot \mathcal{E}_3 f_3\|_{L^1(\R^4)} \les \theta^{-\frac12} \|f\|_{L^2} \|g\|_{L^2} \|h\|_{L^2}, 
\end{equation}
where we use the notation $\mathcal{E} f = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(f \delta_{\Sigma})$ and $\mathcal{F}$ is the (space-time) Fourier transform.

Generically, without assuming any curvature properties for the surfaces $\Sigma_i$, \eqref{triL1} is conjectured to be true in every (space-time) dimension $n \geq 3$ (this is the minimum dimension to have $\theta>0$). In \cite{BeCaTa}, Bennett, Carbery, and Tao proved estimates of this type for $\theta\approx 1$ but with extra subpolynomial losses which make the estimate unusable for the purpose of solving nonlinear PDEs. To get a sense of the difficulty of the sharp endpoint multilinear restriction problem, i.e. the estimate without the loss, one can consider the closely related multilinear Kakeya problem. In \cite{Gu-main}, Guth proved the endpoint $d$-linear Kakeya conjecture in $\R^d$ by using the polynomial method; see also the work of Zhang in \cite{zhang_endpoint_2018} for generalizations of this result.

It may be helpful to explain why \eqref{triL1} and its higher dimensional counterparts are significantly more difficult than the bilinear estimates such as \eqref{bilS}. By Plancherel, the bilinear $L^2$ estimate can be estimated on the Fourier side where the convolution of measures supported in transversal hypersurfaces can be effectively estimated through direct methods leading to \eqref{bilG}. There is no such mechanism for trilinear (or higher order) estimates.  

Based on these considerations, things might look hopeless even for free solutions, let alone the need to transfer the estimates to more general functions in a resolution space. What makes this possible is the observation that the trilinear $L^1$ estimate \eqref{triL1} is ``native" to $\R^3$, while in $\R^4$ (or higher dimensions) curvature can provide some extra room; more precisely, under appropriate transversality and curvature assumptions on the underlying surfaces, the optimal estimate in $\R^4$ is the following
\begin{equation} \label{triLC}
\|\mathcal{E}_1 f_1  \cdot \mathcal{E}_2 f_2 \cdot \mathcal{E}_3 f_3\|_{L^p(\R^4)} \les \|f\|_{L^2} \|g\|_{L^2} \|h\|_{L^2}, 
\end{equation}
for any $p\geq\frac{14}{15}$; note that this estimate is stated here for $\theta \approx 1$. The point is that the estimate is expected to hold true for some $p<1$, and, by interpolating with the trivial $L^\infty$ estimate, one can obtain the desired $L^1$ estimate for free waves without the aforementioned loss.

The estimate as stated in \eqref{triLC} was established by the first author in \cite{Be-mrec}, up to the endpoint, for surfaces satisfying a very general curvature condition. In particular, it allows for the surfaces to have vanishing principal curvatures in some directions, which plays an important role in our current work. However, even the very general formulation available in \cite{Be-mrec} does not provide us with all the necessary tools needed in our analysis and this is why that theory has been augmented in \cite{BeCh} by the first and third authors. 

The next step in the analysis is to transfer this trilinear restriction estimate from free solutions to an appropriate resolution space. A recent paper of the the first author \cite{Be-rs} provides an opening in this direction. There, in the context of the Schr\"odinger equation, a new technique was developed for establishing a transference mechanism of bilinear restriction estimates (available for free solutions) to functions in $V^2_\Delta$ (to be defined in section \ref{s:RS}) with low modulations; most importantly the transference is lossless, unlike what is normally expected. In this paper we deploy a similar argument for transferring trilinear restriction estimates to functions in $V^2_\Delta$ with low modulations. Theorem \ref{thm trilinear transference} is such an example for the Schr\"odinger equation, although for the particular PDE at hand, that is \eqref{QNLS}, we need the more complex version of it provided by Theorem \ref{trilml}. The low modulation interaction captures precisely the problematic resonances, whose structures deliver the necessary geometric structure for deploying the trilinear restriction estimate.

While in this outline we focus on the role of the trilinear restriction estimate, there are also other interactions in \eqref{NXYu2} which need to be controlled. However, armed with this low modulation estimate, we can assume that at least one term in these other interactions has relatively high modulation, which corresponds in some sense to nonresonant interactions. A careful analysis based on linear and bilinear estimates then provides the necessary estimates in these cases.

The grand conclusion of this analysis is that we are able to close the key estimate \eqref{NXYu2} with $X=V^2_\Delta$ (at the level of frequency localized functions). Recalling the standard fact that closing the estimate \eqref{NXYu2} in $V^2_\Delta$ is sufficient for recovering the solution of \eqref{LXY} in the same space $V^2_\Delta$, with the precise formulation being supplied by Theorem \ref{LDUD}, this allows us to close the iteration and prove Theorem \ref{thm gwp}.









\section{The resolution space} \label{s:RS}

In this section we introduce our resolution spaces. We begin with recalling the basic theory of $U^p,V^p$ spaces, followed by their adapted versions $U^p_\Delta,V^p_\Delta$ in the context of the Schr\"odinger flow along with their standard properties. 


\subsection{$U^p$ and $V^p$ spaces} 

In this section we provide a brief introduction of the $U^p,V^p$ spaces; the material here can be found in \cite{KT,HHK,KT1,KT2,CaHe} and the interested reader is advised to consult these papers for any additional details; we would suggest \cite{HHK}, \cite{KTV} and \cite{KT2} as the more comprehensive ones. 


Below we consider intervals $(a,b)$ with $-\infty \leq a < b \leq +\infty$. A partition $\tau$ of $(a,b)$ is a finite monotone sequence $a < t_1 < ...< t_N =b$.

We work with functions $u,v: (a,b) \rightarrow L^2(\R^n)$; as is standard in this context, throughout this paper we work with function $u,v$ that are ruled, in the sense that they have left and right limits everywhere. We also consider $1 \leq  p < \infty$ below. 

\begin{definition}  A $U^p(a,b)$ atom is a step function
\[
a(t)=\sum_{k=1}^K \mathds{1}_{[t_{k-1},t_{k})}(t) \phi_k , \quad \sum_{k=1}^K\|\phi_k\|_{L^2(\R^n)}^p=1,
\]
$U^p(a,b)$ is the atomic space based on these atoms, that is $u \in U^p(a,b)$ if
\[
u = \sum_{j \in \N} \lambda_j a_j,
\]
where $(\lambda_j) \in l^1$ and $a_j$ are atoms as above; $U^p(a,b)$ is equipped with the standard norm
\[
\|u\|_{U^p(a,b)} = \inf \{ \sum_{j \in \N}  |\lambda_j |:  u = \sum_{j \in \N} \lambda_j a_j \ \mbox{as above} \}. 
\]
\end{definition}

By definition, functions $u \in U^p$ are right continuous and $\lim_{t \rightarrow a+} u(t)=0$ in $L^2(\R^n)$. If we allow atoms to include the end-point $t_1=a$, we denote the new space by $\tilde U^p$; note that in this context we lose $\lim_{t \rightarrow a+} u(t)=0$. This is particularly useful when we involve the $U^p$ structure on subintervals of $(a,b)$. In this context we have the following result (see Lemma B.5 in \cite{KT2}):
\begin{equation} \label{U2int}
\| u_1 + u_2 \|^2_{\tilde U^2(a,b)}  \leq \| u_1 \|^2_{\tilde U^2(a,c)} + \| u_2 \|^2_{\tilde U^2(c,b)}, 
\end{equation}
where $c \in (a,b)$. 

\begin{definition} Given a function $v: (a,b) \rightarrow L^2(\R^n)$ we define
\[
|v|_{V^p} =\sup_{\tau=\{(t_j)_{j=1,N}\}} \left( \sum_j \| v(t_{j+1}) -v(t_j) \|_{L^2(\R^n)}^p \right)^\frac1p, 
\quad  \|v\|_{V^p} = |v|_{V^p} + \|v\|_{L^\infty_t L^2_x}. 
\]
We let $V^p=V^p(a,b)$ is the space of functions  $v: (a,b) \rightarrow L^2(\R^n)$ for which $ \|v\|_{V^p}$ is finite. 

\end{definition}

It is easy to show that if $v \in V^p$, then $v$ has lateral limits everywhere, including the end-points $a,b$ (this includes the case $a=-\infty$ and $b=+\infty$); for reference see \cite{HHK}. In order to avoid jumps in $V^2$ that are not related to the lateral limits, it is customary to work with $V^2_{rc}$, the subspace of $V^p$ of functions that are right-continuous, or  $V^2_{lc}$, the subspace of $V^p$ of functions that are left-continuous. In addition, a normalization at one of the end-points is usually present: in $V^2_{rc}$ one uses $\lim_{t \rightarrow a+} v(t)=0$ (in $L^2(\R^n)$), while in $V^2_{lc}$ one uses $\lim_{t \rightarrow b-} v(t)=0$. Such a normalization allows one to skip the use of $\|v\|_{L^\infty_t L^2_x}$ in the definition of $\|\cdot \|_{V^p}$ and simply use only $|v|_{V^p}$. 

In practice, in the context of solutions to nonlinear PDEs, we will have a priori knowledge of the continuity in time of solutions, while the normalization at $-\infty$ or $+\infty$ is unnecessary and incompatible with prescribing an initial data at a specific time $t_0$, which itself acts as a normalization. However one can reach any desired normalization by subtracting a constant in time function. 

Next we continue with basic properties of these spaces. We record the embeddings
\begin{equation}
U^p \subset V^p \subset U^q, \quad  1 \leq p < q < \infty;
\end{equation}
the first embedding is straightforward for atoms, hence for the whole $U^p$, while the second requires a bit more work, see for instance \cite{HHK}, Proposition 2.5 and Corollary 2.6 for a complete argument. 

As a consequence of Proposition 2.5 in \cite{HHK}, we have that given $v \in V^2$, $q>2$ and $N \in \N$, there exists a decomposition $v=u_N + v-u_N$ such that 
such that
\begin{equation} \label{V2U2q}
(\ln N)^{-1} \|u_N\|_{U^2} + 2^{N(1-\frac2q)} \|v-u_N\|_{U^q} \leq C(q) \|v\|_{V^2}. 
\end{equation}
This is formalized in the body of the proof of Proposition 2.20 in \cite{HHK}. Moreover, any frequency localization properties on the original $v$ is inherited by $u_N$ as well; to be more precise, if $P(D_x)$ is a frequency localization operator and $P(D_x) u(t) = u(t), \forall t$, then $P(D_x) u_N(t) = u_N(t), \forall t$. 
This property is obvious from the construction of $u_N$ in Proposition 2.5 in \cite{HHK}. 


It is important to understand how the $U^p$ and $V^p$ structures interact with decompositions which have good orthogonality properties; we use the following result from Proposition 4.3 in \cite{CaHe}.
\begin{proposition} \label{Pref}
Let $M_1,M_2 \in (0,+\infty)$. For $k \in \N$, let $T_k : L^2(\R^n) \rightarrow L^2(\R^n)$ be a linear and bounded operator (acting spatially) such that
\[
M_1^{-1} \| \sum_{k \in \N}  T_k f \|_{L^2} \leq \left( \sum_{k \in \N}  \|T_k f \|^2_{L^2} \right)^\frac12 \leq M_2 \|f\|_{L^2}, \quad \forall f \in L^2.
\]
If $1 \leq p \leq 2$, then for all $u \in U^p$ we have the bound
\begin{equation} \label{U2dec}
\left( \sum_{k \in \N}  \|T_k u \|^2_{U^p} \right)^\frac12 \leq M_2 \|u\|_{U^p}.
\end{equation}
If $ p \geq 2$, then for all $v \in V^p$ we have the bound
\begin{equation} \label{V2dec}
 \| \sum_{k \in \N}  T_k v \|_{V^p}  \leq M_1 \left( \sum_{k \in \N}  \|T_k v \|^2_{V^p} \right)^\frac12. 
