\begin{abstract}
Compactified Imaginary Liouville Theory (CILT for short) is a probabilistically constructed Conformal Field Theory (CFT), expected to have connections to minimal models, Coulomb gas integrals, and loop models. In this paper, we study the quantization of Segal's semigroup given by CILT. Each element $\mathbf T_f$ in the quantized semigroup corresponds to a conformal annulus: the unit disk $\mathbb D$ with $f(\mathbb D^\circ)$ removed, where $f:\mathbb D\to f(\mathbb D)\subset \mathbb D^\circ$ is a contracting biholomorphism. This semigroup encodes the local conformal structure of the CFT, in particular the Virasoro symmetry with central charge $<1$ for CILT. We first relate the quantized Segal's semigroup to Segal's amplitudes of annuli and recover the Virasoro generators $\mathbf L_n,\widetilde{\mathbf L}_n$ ($n\geq0$) of CILT as generators of this semigroup. Compared to the real Liouville CFT, the main difficulty here is the absence of positivity, which we address through estimates for the imaginary Gaussian Multiplicative Chaos and non-self-adjoint operators. Using Segal's gluing axiom as in the real case, we differentiate the amplitude and use conformal covariance to derive the Ward identities in terms of all Virasoro operators $\mathbf L_n,\widetilde{\mathbf L}_n$ ($n\in\mathbb Z$). We also outline the parallel theory for Boundary CILT (BCILT), concerning Segal's semigroup of semiannuli. These results provide essential tools for the conformal bootstrap program of (B)CILT, boundary BPZ equations, boundary structure constants, and the global construction of conformal blocks.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
The Compactified Imaginary Liouville Theory (CILT for short) is a 2-dimensional Conformal Field Theory (CFT) defined on a Riemann surface $\Sigma$ with a conformal metric $g$, whose correlation functions can be formally written as a path integral of the form
\[\la F(\phi)\ra_{\Sigma,g}:=\int F(\phi)\,e^{-S_\L(\phi)}\,\D\phi,\]
where $F(\phi)$ is a suitable test functional and $\D\phi$ is the formal ``Lebesgue'' measure on the space of maps $\phi$ from $\Sigma$ to the circle $\mathbb R/2\pi R\mathbb Z$ of radius $R>0$. If $\Sigma$ has boundary, we require that $\phi$ satisfies the Neumann boundary condition at $\pa\Sigma$. Here the \textit{imaginary Liouville action} $S_{\rm L}$ is formally given by (without the last two terms if $\partial\Sigma=\emptyset$)
\begin{equation*}
    S_\L(\phi)=
\frac{1}{4\pi}\int_{\Sigma}|d\phi|_g^2\,\d v_g
+\frac{\i Q}{4\pi}\int_\Sigma K_g\phi\,\d v_g
+\mu\int_\Sigma e^{\i \beta\phi}\,\d v_g+\frac{\i Q}{2\pi}\int_{\pa\Sigma}k_g\phi\,\d\ell_g+\int_{\pa\Sigma}\mu_\pa\,e^{\i\frac{\beta}{2}\phi}\,\d\ell_g,
\end{equation*}
where $Q,\beta\in\mathbb R$ with $\beta^2\in(0,2)$, $\mu\in\mathbb C$, $\mu_\partial:\pa\Sigma\to\Cb$ is a piecewise constant function, and $K_g$, $v_g$, $k_g$, $\ell_g$ denote respectively the scalar curvature, volume, geodesic curvature, and length with respect to $g$.
When $\mu=0$, $\mu_\partial=0$, this becomes the Coulomb gas formalism (see \cite{dotsenko1984conformal,dotsenko1985four,Francesco1987,Nienhuis1987}). Throughout this paper, we impose the relation
\[Q=\frac\beta2-\frac2\beta.\]
The well-definedness of the Liouville action requires that $\beta R, QR\in\Zb$ if $\partial\Sigma=\emptyset$ and $\beta R, QR\in2\Zb$ if $\partial\Sigma\not=\emptyset$. These conditions force $\beta^2\in\Qb$, and given such $\beta$, one can always choose $R$ accordingly. Then this path integral defines a CFT with central charge $c_{\mathrm L}=1-6Q^2$.
The mathematical construction of this CFT was performed in \cite{CILT} for the case without boundary, and later the boundary case, called Boundary CILT (BCILT for short), was studied in \cite{BCILT}. In physics, there have been many related research directions as in \cite{Schomerus2003,Zamolodchikov2005,Kostov2006,Harlow2011,Ribault2015,Usciati2025}, aiming either to capture the ``imaginary'' version of the real Liouville theory (proposed in \cite{Polyakov1981}), or to formulate a CFT candidate for the 2-dimensional loop models, see for instance \cite{Francesco1987,Nienhuis1987,Kondev1996,Ikhlef2016,Nivesvivat2024}. On the mathematical side, there are also related works such as \cite{Ang21,Baverez2024,Chatterjee2026}.  

In BCILT, we consider correlation functions of vertex operators of the form
\begin{equation*}
   \prod_{j=1}^\s V_{\alpha_j}(z_j)\prod_{j=1}^\t V_{\eta_j}(x_j),\ \ \ z_j\in\Sigma^\circ,\ x_j\in\partial\Sigma,\ \ \ \s,\t\in\mathbb N
\end{equation*}
with $V_{\alpha_j}(z_j):=e^{\i\alpha_j\phi(z_j)}$ and $V_{\eta_j}(x_j):=e^{\i\frac{\eta_j}2\phi(x_j)}$, called \textit{electric operators}, in the interior (or \textit{bulk}) $\Sigma^\circ$ and on the boundary $\partial \Sigma$, respectively. % (they need regularizations since $\phi$ is a distribution).
In addition to these, there is another type of operators known as \textit{magnetic operators}, defined only in the bulk. Specifically, for ${\bf z}=(z_1,\ldots,z_\s)\in(\Sigma^\circ)^\s$ and \textit{magnetic charges} ${\bf m}=(m_1,\ldots,m_\s)\in\Zb^\s$, we define formally
\begin{equation*}
    \left\langle F(\phi)\prod_{j=1}^\s V_{(0,m_j)}(z_j)\right\rangle_{\Sigma,g}:=\int F(\phi+2\pi R\,I_{\zbf,\mbf})\,e^{-S_\L(\phi+2\pi R\,I_{\zbf,\mbf})}\,\D\phi,
\end{equation*}
where $I_{\zbf,\mbf}(z):=\int_{x_0}^z\nu_{\zbf,\mbf}$ (with fixed basepoint $x_0\in\Sigma$) and $\nu_{\zbf,\mbf}$ is a chosen $1$-form that equals $\frac{m_j}{2\pi} \, d\theta$ in a local coordinate $z=z_j+r e^{\i\theta}$ near $z_j$ for $j=1,\cdots,\s$. If $\pa\Sigma=\emptyset$, such a $1$-form exists if and only if $\sum_{j=1}^{\s} m_j=0$.
We remark that here both the field $\phi$ and $2\pi R\,I_{\zbf,\mbf}$ take values in the circle $\Tb_R:=\Rb/2\pi R\Zb$, so they are multi-valued as functions to $\Rb$. In order to make sense of the curvature terms $\frac{\i Q}{4\pi}\int_\Sigma K_g\phi\,\d v_g+\frac{\i Q}{2\pi}\int_{\pa\Sigma}k_g\phi\,\d\ell_g$ in the Liouville functional, a special regularization procedure is needed. This is done by fixing a ``branch cut'' of the surface, called \textit{separating family} in \cite{BCILT} (the notion of \textit{defect graph} in \cite{CILT} is a special case thereof), and adding appropriate regularization terms at the cuts. It can be shown that the result does not depend on the choice of the separating family, up to an integral multiple of $2\pi R$.
Finally, one can combine the electric and magnetic operators to form the \textit{electromagnetic operators}, by taking a limit $z_j'\to z_j$ in the direction specified by a nonzero tangent vector $v_j$ at $z_j$ and defining $V_{(\alpha_j,m_j)}(v_j):=\lim_{z_j'\to z_j}V_{\alpha_j}(z_j')V_{(0,m_j)}(z_j)$.
The operators $V_{(\alpha_j,m_j)}(v_j)$ and $V_{\eta_j}(x_j)$ give the primary states of BCILT with conformal weights $\Delta_{\alpha_j,m_j}$ and $\Delta_{\eta_j}$, where
\[\Delta_{\alpha,m}:=\frac\alpha2\left(\frac\alpha2-Q\right)+\frac{m^2R^2}4,\qquad\Delta_\eta:=\frac{\eta}{2}\left(\frac\eta2-Q\right).\]
For more background on BCILT, including its rigorous construction, we refer to \cite{CILT,BCILT}.

Given a CFT, a central mathematical problem is to establish the conformal bootstrap rigorously. The BPZ proposal \cite{Belavin1984}, together with Segal's gluing axioms \cite{Segal1988}, provides a guideline for recovering correlation functions from amplitudes and conformal blocks. In the case of the real Liouville CFT, ever since its probabilistic construction on general surfaces in \cite{Guillarmou2019}, there has been significant progress in this direction. In particular, the bootstrap factorization formula was proved for the $4$-point function on the sphere in \cite{Guillarmou2024} and for general surfaces in \cite{Guillarmou2021}. For the boundary Liouville CFT, the conformal bootstrap was also solved in \cite{boundary_bootstrap}. A natural next step is to carry out the corresponding bootstrap program for BCILT. Let us illustrate this with the simplest example of the $4$-point function on the sphere. First recall that the conformal covariance reduces the $3$-point function on the Riemann sphere $(\hat{\mathbb C},g_0)$ with $g_0=\frac{|\d z|^2}{\max(|z|,1)^4}$ to a constant, known as the \textit{structure constant}. In CILT, it is given by (assuming the neutrality condition $2Q-\sum_j\alpha_j\in\beta\Nb$)
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
    &\left\langle V_{(\alpha_1,m_1)}(0,e_1)V_{(\alpha_2,m_2)}(1,e_1)V_{(\alpha_3,m_3)}(\infty,e_1) \right\rangle_{\hat{\mathbb C},g_0}^2\\
    &= C_{g_0}^2 4\pi^2 R^2 \mu^{\frac2\beta(2Q-\sum_{j=1}^3 \alpha_j)} C_{\beta}^{\mathrm{DOZZ}}(\alpha_1+m_1R,\alpha_2+m_2R,\alpha_3+m_3R)\,C_{\beta}^{\mathrm{DOZZ}}(\alpha_1-m_1R,\alpha_2-m_2R,\alpha_3-m_3R),
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
where $C_{g_0}$ is a universal constant, $C_{\beta}^{\mathrm{DOZZ}}(\cdot,\cdot,\cdot)$ is the \textit{imaginary DOZZ constant}, and $e_1=\partial_x$ in the coordinate $z=x+\i y$ in $\mathbb C$. This formula appeared in \cite{Zamolodchikov2005,Kostov2006}, and it was proved for CILT based on \cite{Neretin2024}, see \cite[Section 7]{CILT}. Now for the $4$-point function on the sphere, we expect a factorization formula of the form (again assuming the neutrality condition $2Q-\sum_j\alpha_j\in\beta\Nb$)
\begin{equation}\label{eq_bootstrap}
\left\langle V_{(\alpha_1,m_1)}(0,e_1)V_{(\alpha_2,m_2)}(z,e_1) V_{(\alpha_3,m_3)}(1,e_1)V_{(\alpha_4,m_4)}(\infty,e_1) \right\rangle_{\hat{\mathbb C},g_0}
    =\sum_{n=0}^{\frac{2Q-\sum_j\alpha_j}\beta}\rho(\alphabf,\mbf,n)\mathcal F_{\alphabf,\mbf,n}(z)\widetilde{\mathcal F}_{\alphabf,\mbf,n}(\bar z),
\end{equation}
where $z\in\hat\Cb\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}$,
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
     \rho(\alphabf,\mbf,n):={}&\left\langle V_{(\alpha_1,m_1)}(0,e_1)V_{(\alpha_2,m_2)}(1,e_1)V_{(2Q-\alpha_1-\alpha_2-n\beta,-m_1-m_2)}(\infty,e_1) \right\rangle_{\hat{\mathbb C},g_0}\\
     &\times\left\langle V_{(\alpha_1+\alpha_2+n\beta,m_1+m_2)}(0,e_1) V_{(\alpha_3,m_3)}(1,e_1)V_{(\alpha_4,m_4)}(\infty,e_1)\right\rangle_{\hat{\mathbb C},g_0},
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
and $\mathcal F_{\alphabf,\mbf,n}(z),\widetilde{\mathcal F}_{\alphabf,\mbf,n}(\bar z)$ are the holomorphic and anti-holomorphic Virasoro conformal blocks. The existence of spin means that CILT is \textit{nondiagonal}, and thus the two conformal blocks $\mathcal F_{\alphabf,\mbf,n},\widetilde{\mathcal F}_{\alphabf,\mbf,n}$ are distinct; this is also seen in the Virasoro actions in Section \ref{sec_Virasoro}. The formula \eqref{eq_bootstrap} is only formally written since both the structure constants and the conformal blocks can have poles, and the existence of these poles is a consequence of the complicated algebraic structure, in particular the logarithmic Jordan partners and the degenerate Shapovalov forms. Understanding these poles would be the last step towards the solution of the $4$-point function on the sphere. In a companion work, the first author, together with C. Guillarmou and R. Rhodes, will handle the $4$-point function assuming $\beta^2\notin\mathbb Q$ or $(\alpha_1+\alpha_2-Q)\beta\not\in \mathbb Q$, which would save us from the algebraic problems encountered in the proof of the bootstrap formula.

In this paper, we provide the prerequisite theory for the conformal bootstrap, namely the quantization of Segal's semigroup of annuli given by (B)CILT. We prove differentiability of the quantized semigroup, identify its infinitesimal generators and use this to derive the Ward identities for (B)CILT amplitudes. Analogously to the real counterpart \cite{Segal_semigroup}, these results provide the main tools for the construction of the conformal block and the proof of its Ward identities in the case of (B)CILT. Besides, this paper is also a part of the proof of the BPZ equations in (B)CILT, which will be done in a future study of higher equations of motion as in the real case \cite{Baverez2023} (see also \cite{Cercle2025}). The boundary BPZ equations will play an important role in the study of the boundary structure constants proposed in BCILT \cite[Section 8]{BCILT}. There has been a very recent paper \cite{Cercle2026} on the BPZ equations and Ward identities (for correlation functions only) of the non-compactified imaginary Liouville theory, which also covers those of CILT, at least if there are only electric operators.

We now give an overview of our main results. First we review the definition of Segal amplitudes in (B)CILT. For simplicity, consider the case of CILT, i.e., assume that there is no Neumann boundary. According to Segal's axiomatization of CFT \cite{Segal1988}, we associate to each Riemannian surface with parametrized boundary an amplitude operator between tensor products of a Hilbert space $\mathcal H$, called the \textit{state space} of the CFT. In CILT, the Hilbert space $\mathcal H$ is the space of $L^2$ functionals on the space of $\Rb/2\pi R\Zb$-valued boundary fields $\wt{\varphi}^k$ on a boundary circle, having the form
\begin{equation*}
    \widetilde{\varphi}^k(\theta) = c + \varphi(\theta) + kR\theta,
\end{equation*}
where $c\in\mathbb R/2\pi R\mathbb Z,k\in\mathbb Z$, and $\varphi$ is the mean-zero Gaussian Free Field (GFF)
\begin{equation*}
    \varphi(\theta)=\sum_{n\not=0}\varphi_n e^{\i n\theta},
\end{equation*}
where $\varphi_n=\frac{x_n+\i y_n}{2\sqrt{n}}$ and $\varphi_{-n}=\overline{\varphi}_n$ for $n>0$, and $x_n,y_n$ are i.i.d.\ standard Gaussians. We equip the space of $(c,k,\varphi)$ with the product of the Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb R/2\pi R\mathbb Z$, the counting measure on $\mathbb Z$, and the Gaussian probability measure on the component $\varphi$, i.e., %Namely, the Hilbert space $\mathcal H$ can be written as the $L^2$ space on $\mathbb R/2\pi R\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z\times(\mathbb R^2)^{\mathbb N^*}$ equipped with measure (see \cref{Sec_background} for rigorous explanation)
\begin{equation}\label{eq_defn_phik}
\d\widetilde{\varphi}^k:=\d c\otimes\left(\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}\delta_k\right)\otimes\bigotimes_{n\geq1}\frac{1}{2\pi}e^{-\frac{x^2_n+y_n^2}{2}}\,\d x_n\d y_n.
\end{equation}
See \cref{Sec_background} for details. Then an $L^2$ functional of $\bt^\kbf=(\wt{\varphi}_1^{k_1},\ldots,\wt{\varphi}_\b^{k_\b})$ lives in the tensor product $\mathcal H^{\otimes \b}$. 
Now let $\Sigma$ be a Riemann surface with conformal metric $g$ and boundary parametrizations $\zetabf=(\zeta_1,\cdots,\zeta_\b)$, where each $\zeta_j:\Tb=\{e^{\i\theta}:\theta\in\Rb\}\to\pa_j\Sigma$ is a real-analytic parametrization of a boundary circle $\pa_j\Sigma$. 
Here the orientation of each parametrization in $\zetabf$ is either incoming or outgoing, and we denote by $\b^+$ (resp.\ $\b^-$) the number of outgoing (resp.\ incoming) boundary circles, with $\b=\b^++\b^-$. 
In this paper, the metric $g$ will be assumed to be  \textit{admissible} in the sense that $\zeta_j^*g=\frac{|\mathrm{d}z|^2}{|z|^2}$ near each boundary circle. 
Let $\zbf=(z_1,\cdots,z_\s)$ be distinct marked points in $\Sigma^\circ$, $\vbf=(v_1,\cdots,v_\s)$ where each $v_j$ is a nonzero tangent vector at $z_j$, $\alphabf=(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\s)$ electric charges, and $\mbf=(m_1,\ldots,m_\s)$ magnetic charges. Then the CILT \textit{amplitude} of $(\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf)$ is given by the formal path integral
\[\Ac_{\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf}(F,\bt^\kbf):=
\int F(\phi)\prod_{j=1}^\s V_{(\alpha_j,m_j)}(v_j)\,e^{-S_\L(\phi)}\,\D\phi,\]
where the integral is over maps $\phi:\Sigma\setminus\{z_1,\ldots,z_\s\}\to\Rb/2\pi R\Zb$ satisfying $\zetabf^*\phi|_{\pa\Sigma}=\bt^\kbf=(\wt{\varphi}_1^{k_1},\ldots,\wt{\varphi}_\b^{k_\b})$ and having winding number $m_j\in\Zb$ around each $z_j$. Here $\kbf=(k_1,\cdots,k_{\b})$ are thus the winding numbers of $\phi$ on the Dirichlet boundary $\pa\Sigma$. For the rigorous definition of this path integral, we refer to \cite[Section 8]{CILT}. One can show that, under suitable conditions, the functional $\Ac_{\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf}(F,\cdot)$ is $L^2$ and can thus be viewed as the kernel of a Hilbert--Schmidt operator
\begin{equation*}
    \Ac_{\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf}(F):\mathcal H^{\otimes\b^-}\to\mathcal H^{\otimes\b^+}.
\end{equation*}
In the case $F\equiv1$, we shall simply write $\Ac_{\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf}$, or even $\mathcal A_{\Sigma,g,\zetabf}$ if there are no marked points. 
These amplitudes are diffeomorphism-invariant and satisfy conformal covariance properties under a conformal change of the metric $g$. Moreover, they satisfy the following gluing property. If $(\Sigma^\ell,g^\ell,\vbf^\ell,\alphabf^\ell,\mbf^\ell,\zetabf^\ell)$ ($\ell=1,2$) are two such surfaces with $\zeta_{j_1}^1$ incoming and $\zeta_{j_2}^2$ outgoing, then we can glue them into a new surface $(\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf)$ by setting $\zeta^1_{j_1}(e^{\i\theta})=\zeta^2_{j_2}(e^{\i\theta})$ for $\theta\in\Rb$. Then we have
\begin{equation*}
    \Ac_{\Sigma,g,\vbf,\alphabf,\mbf,\zetabf}=C\Ac_{\Sigma^1,g^1,\vbf^1,\alphabf^1,\mbf^1,\zetabf^1}\circ_{j_1,j_2}\Ac_{\Sigma^2,g^2,\vbf^2,\alphabf^2,\mbf^2,\zetabf^2},
\end{equation*}
where $C$ is a universal constant and $\circ_{j_1,j_2}$ denotes the composition (or trace) of the $j_1$-th tensor component of $\Ac_{\Sigma^1,g^1,\vbf^1,\alphabf^1,\mbf^1,\zetabf^1}$ with the $j_2$-th tensor component of $\Ac_{\Sigma^2,g^2,\vbf^2,\alphabf^2,\mbf^2,\zetabf^2}$. For the proofs of these claims, we refer again to \cite{CILT,BCILT}.



Now we introduce Segal's semigroup of annuli, appearing first in \cite[Section 2]{Segal1988} and independently in \cite{Neretin1987,Neretin1990}. The real analog of the following quantization for the real Liouville CFT was done in \cite{Segal_semigroup} and later \cite[Section 7.3]{boundary_bootstrap} for the boundary case. 
Let $\Db=\{|z|\leq1\}$ be the closed unit disk, and consider the semigroup $\Sc$ of holomorphic embeddings $f:\Db\to\Db^\circ$ with $f(0) = 0$ such that it extends holomorphically to a neighborhood of the boundary $\pa\Db=\mathbb T$. For $f\in\Sc$, consider the annulus $\mathbb A_{f}:=\mathbb D\setminus f(\mathbb D^\circ)$ with boundary parametrizations $\zetabf_f:=(\id_\Tb,f|_\Tb)$. Then for a functional $F(\wt{\varphi}^k)$ in an appropriate space (see \eqref{eq_def_E_space}), we define
\begin{equation*}
(\Tbf_fF)(\wt{\varphi}^k) := |f'(0)|^{\frac{R^2k^2-Q^2}2} \,\Eb_{\wt{\varphi}^k}\left[F\left((P\wt{\varphi}^k+X_\Db)\circ f\big|_\Tb + \i Q\log\tfrac{|f'|}{|f|}\big|_\Tb\right)\,e^{-\mu\int_{\Ab_f}|x|^{\beta Q}M_\beta(P\wt{\varphi}^k+X_\Db,\d x)}\right],
\end{equation*}
where $P\wt{\varphi}^k$ is the harmonic extension of $\widetilde{\varphi}^k$ to $\mathbb D$ (with prescribed singularity at $0$) and $X_{\mathbb D}$ is the Dirichlet GFF on $\mathbb D$. The conditional expectation $\Eb_{\wt{\varphi}^k}$ averages over the Dirichlet GFF $X_{\mathbb D}$. Here $M_{\beta}$ is the imaginary Gaussian Multiplicative Chaos (GMC, see \cite{Lacoin2015}) defined as follows. For a general field $\phi_g$ on a Riemannian surface $(\Sigma,g)$, we define (see \cref{Sec_background} for details)
\begin{equation*}
    M_{\beta}(\phi_g,\d v_g):=\lim_{\epsilon\to 0} \epsilon^{-\frac{\beta^2}{2}}e^{\i\beta \phi_{g,\epsilon}(x)}\d v_g,
\end{equation*}
where $\phi_{g,\epsilon}$ is an $\epsilon$-regularization with respect to $g$. 
We prove that $\Tbf_f$ extends to a bounded operator $\Hc\to\Hc$, and that this coincides (up to a constant) with the amplitude $\Ac_{\Ab_f,g_f,\zetabf_f}$ of the annulus $(\Ab_f,\zetabf_f)$ equipped with an admissible metric $g_f$ (see below). The homomorphism $f\in\Sc\mapsto\Tbf_f$ provides a quantization of Segal's semigroup $\Sc$ into operators acting on $\Hc$.

The operator $\Tbf_f$ can be used to describe the local conformal symmetry of CILT. In particular, differentiating $\Tbf_f$ gives the Virasoro actions of CILT. An important special case is that the generator of the semigroup $t\in\Rb_{>0}\mapsto\mathbf T_{z\mapsto e^{-t}z}$ is the \textit{Hamiltonian} of CILT, given by (using the decomposition in \eqref{eq_defn_phik})
\begin{equation}\label{eq_def_H}
    \mathbf H:=-\frac12\partial_c^2+\frac{R^2k^2-Q^2}2+2\sum_{n\geq1}(\mathbf A^*_n\mathbf A_n+\widetilde{\mathbf A}_n^*\widetilde{\mathbf A}_n)+\mu\, e^{\i\beta c}\int_0^{2\pi} e^{\i\beta Rk\theta}\,e^{\i\beta\varphi(\theta)}\,\d\theta,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf A_n:=\tfrac\i2\sqrt n(\partial_{x_n}-\i\partial_{y_n})$, $\widetilde{\mathbf A}_n:=\tfrac\i2\sqrt n(\partial_{x_n}+\i\partial_{y_n})$, and $\int_0^{2\pi} e^{\i\beta Rk\theta}\,e^{\i\beta\varphi(\theta)}\,\d\theta$ is a boundary GMC (see \cref{Sec_Virasoro_Liouville}). Note that $\mathbf H$ is \textit{not} self-adjoint, unlike the real case. 
In general, we can consider the flow $t\in\Rb_{\geq0}\mapsto f_t\in\mathcal S$ generated by a Markovian holomorphic vector field $\v=v(z)\partial_z=-\sum_{n\geq0}v_n z^{n+1}\partial_z$ on $\mathbb D$, i.e., we assume that $v(0)=0$ and $\Re(\zb v(z))<0$ for $z\in\Tb$, and we solve the ordinary differential equation $\partial_t f_t(z)=v(f_t(z))$ for $z\in\Db$ with $f_0=\id_\Db$. Then the generator of the semigroup $t\in\Rb_{>0}\mapsto\mathbf T_{f_t}$, denoted $\mathbf H_\v$, is given by
\begin{equation}\label{intro_H_v}
    \mathbf H_\v:=\sum_{n\geq0}v_n\,\Lbf_n+\sum_{n\geq0}\ol{v_n}\,\wt{\Lbf}_n,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf L_n,\widetilde{\mathbf L}_n$ are generators of the holomorphic and anti-holomorphic Virasoro algebras defined by CILT. They enjoy explicit formulas \eqref{eq_L} in terms of $\mathbf A_n,\widetilde{\mathbf A}_n$ and the boundary GMC. %The Virasoro comutation relation holds for the free field algebra ($\mu=0$).

We are ready to state our first result on the connection between the annulus amplitude and Virasoro generators:
\begin{thm}\label{prop_intro}
\mbox{}
   \begin{enumerate}[label=\emph{(\arabic*)}]
       \item For $f\in\Sc$, the operator $\Tbf_f$ extends to a bounded operator $\Hc\to\Hc$, and the map $f\in\Ssg\mapsto\Tbf_f$ is a semigroup homomorphism, i.e., for $f_1,f_2\in\Ssg$,
        \begin{equation*}
            \Tbf_{f_1}\circ\Tbf_{f_2}=\Tbf_{f_1\circ f_2}.
        \end{equation*}
       \item  Let $f\in\Ssg$, and let $g_f$ be an admissible metric on the annulus $(\Ab_f=\Db\setminus f(\Db^\circ),\zetabf_f=(\id_\Tb,f|_\Tb))$. Viewing the CILT amplitude $\Ac_{\Ab_f,g_f,\zetabf_f}$ of $(\Ab_f,g_f,\zetabf_f)$ as an operator $\mathcal H\to\mathcal H$, we have
\[\Tbf_f = \frac{e^{-\frac{c_{\mathrm L}}{12} W(f, g_f)}}{\sqrt{2}\pi} \Ac_{\Ab_f,g_f,\zetabf_f},\]
where $W(f,g_f)$ is defined in \eqref{eq_W_f} and depends on $f$ and $g_f$ only.
\item \emph{(\textbf{Feynman--Kac formula})} Let $\v = -\sum_{n=0}^\infty v_n z^{n+1} \pa_z$ be a Markovian holomorphic vector field on $\Db$ such that the coercivity condition \eqref{omega_condition} holds. Then the operator $\Hbf_{\v}$ admits a closed extension that generates a strongly continuous semigroup $t\in\Rb_{\geq0}\mapsto e^{-t\Hbf_\v}$, and we have $\Tbf_{f_t} = e^{-t\Hbf_\v}$ for $t>0$, where $t\mapsto f_t$ is the flow generated by $\v$.
   \end{enumerate}
\end{thm}
When proving the Feynman--Kac formula, we have fewer tools compared to the real case where the GMC potential is positive. We comment on the technical difficulty in \cref{rmk_tech_GMC}. In short, we need uniform exponential-moment estimates for a sequence of GMC renormalizations compatible with the Hilbert space $\mathcal H$.

\cref{prop_intro} is proved in \cref{prop_semigroup_as_amplitude,T_f_semigroup,prop_FK_formula}. In the course of the proof, we will consider the positive quadratic form $\widetilde{\mathcal Q}^0(F,G):=\la(\mathbf H^0+Q^2)F,G\ra_{L^2}$, where $\mathbf H^0$ is the free Hamiltonian obtained by setting $\mu=0$ in the formula of $\mathbf H$ in \eqref{eq_def_H}. We refer to \cite[Section 4.2]{Guillarmou2024} which uses an analogous quadratic form. Let $\mathcal D(\widetilde{\mathcal Q}^0 )\subset\mathcal H$ be the domain of $\widetilde{\mathcal Q}^0$, and $\Dc'(\wt{\Qc}^0)\supset\Hc$ its dual. We now state our second result on the differentiability of $\Tbf_f$ in $f$:

\begin{thm}\label{thm_rep_segalsemigroup}
The map $f\in\Ssg\mapsto \Tbf_f$ is differentiable with differential
\[D_\v\Tbf_f = -\Tbf_f\circ\Hbf_{\rm w},\qquad {\rm w} = \tfrac{v(z)}{f'(z)}\,\pa_z,\]
where $\v=v(z)\pa_z$ is any holomorphic vector field on $\Db$ with $v(0)=0$. More precisely, given a differentiable family $t\mapsto f_t\in\Ssg$ with $f_0=f$ and $\pa_t|_{t=0}f_t=\v$ (in the sense that $\pa_t|_{t=0}f_t(z)=v(z)$ for $z\in\Db$), we have
\[\lim_{t\to0}\left\|\frac{\Tbf_{f_t}F-\Tbf_fF}{t} +\Tbf_f\Hbf_{\rm w}F\right\|_{\Dc(\wt{\Qc}^0)}=0,\qquad\fr F\in\Dc(\wt{\Qc}^0).\]
\end{thm}
The proof is done by adapting the differentiability framework of the real theory \cite[Theorem 4.8]{Segal_semigroup} to the non-self-adjoint generators. We propose \cref{assmp_diff} as a modification of \cite[Assumption 4.7]{Segal_semigroup} and prove \cref{prop_general_T_f_diff}.  The main difficulty of the proof is to show that $f\mapsto\mathbf T_f$ is continuous in $f$ (\cref{prop_Tf_continuity}), which again involves estimates of the imaginary GMC.

Note that the operators so far involve only $\mathbf L_n,\widetilde{\mathbf L}_n$ for $n\geq0$, and this is because we are only considering annuli $\Ab_f$ where $f$ extends holomorphically to $\Db$. In the case of a general annulus, we can use the same conformal welding argument as in the real case (\cite[Section 4.3]{Segal_semigroup}) to differentiate its amplitude, which will involve $\mathbf L_n,\widetilde{\mathbf L}_n$ for all $n\in\Zb$; see \cref{annulus_differential} for the precise statement. By the gluing axiom, we then know how to differentiate the amplitude of a general surface under a deformation of its boundary parametrizations; see \cref{fig_cutannuli}. As an application, we derive the Ward identities for the CILT amplitudes:

