\section{Background and notations}\label{Sec_background}

For a Hilbert space $\Hc$, we denote by $\Lc(\Hc)$ the space of bounded linear operators on $\Hc$.

We denote by $\Db:=\{z\in\Cb:|z|\leq1\}$ the closed unit disk and by $\Tb:=\pa\Db=\{z\in\Cb:|z|=1\}$ the unit circle. With $\Hb:=\{z\in\Cb:\Im z \geq0\}$ denoting the closed upper half plane, we denote by $\Db^+:=\Db\cap\Hb$ the closed unit half disk and by $\Tb^+:=\Tb\cap\Hb$ the unit half circle. A map defined on a closed set in a Riemann surface is said to be holomorphic if it extends to a holomorphic map on an open neighborhood. In particular, we shall speak of holomorphic maps on $\Db$ and $\Tb$. These will extend to holomorphic maps on $\{|z|<1+\delta\}$ and $\{(1+\delta)^{-1}<|z|<1+\delta\}$, respectively, for some $\delta>0$.

Throughout the paper, we fix a compactification radius $R>0$ and write $\TR:=\Rb/2\pi R\Zb$. Let us recall the measure spaces of $\TR$-valued boundary fields defined in \cite{BCILT}. For the unit circle $\Tb$, we consider the space
\[\DT = \TR \times \Dc_0'(\Tb,\Rb) \times \Zb,\]
with the corresponding decomposition of an element therein
\[\widetilde{\varphi}^k = c + \varphi + kR\arg,\]
understood intuitively as a $\Tb_R$-valued random distribution on $\Tb$ with winding number $k$. (In the notation of \cite{CILT}, the space is $H^s_{\mathbb Z}(\mathbb R)/\langle\tau_{R}\rangle\cong \TR \times H^s_0(\Tb,\Rb) \times \Zb$, defined in terms of the Sobolev space $H^s$ of fixed negative order $s<0$. This differs from our space only by a set of measure zero.) Here the mean-zero component $\varphi$ is sampled from the circle GFF (viewing functions on $\Tb$ as

