\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Approximable triangulated categories, and their weak variant, were introduced by Neeman \cite{Neeman:2021c,Neeman:2021,Neeman:2026}.
The theory has been used to settle conjectures of Bondal--Van den Bergh \cite{Neeman:2021} and of Antieau--Gepner--Heller \cite{Neeman:2024}.
It has also found applications to Fourier--Mukai theory for singular schemes  \cite{Dutta/Lank/ManaliRahul:2025,GuisadoVillaalgordo/Lank/ManaliRahul/Pavic:2025,GuisadoVillaalgordo/Lank:2026}.
See \cite{Canonaco/Neeman/Stellari:2024} for a survey of recent developments.

Roughly speaking, a triangulated category with small coproducts and a compact generator $G$ is weakly approximable if every object in the aisle of a suitable $t$-structure can be suitably approximated from small coproducts of a uniformly bounded range of shifts of $G$.
For example, if $X$ is a quasi-compact quasi-separated scheme and $Z\subseteq X$ is a closed subset with quasi-compact complement, $D_{\operatorname{qc},Z}(X)$ is weakly approximable \cite[Theorem 3.2(iv)]{Neeman:2024}.

The main result is the following. 

\begin{theorem}
    \label{thm:glueing_weak_approx}
    Consider a recollement of singly compactly generated triangulated categories:
    \begin{displaymath}
        \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=6em]
            {\mathcal{D}_Z} & {\mathcal{D}} & {\mathcal{D}_U.}
            \arrow["{i_\ast}" description, from=1-1, to=1-2]
            \arrow["{i^\ast}"', bend right=35, from=1-2, to=1-1]
            \arrow["{i^!}", bend left=35, from=1-2, to=1-1]
            \arrow["{j^\ast}" description, from=1-2, to=1-3]
            \arrow["{j_!}"', bend right=35, from=1-3, to=1-2]
            \arrow["{j_\ast}", bend left=35, from=1-3, to=1-2]
        \end{tikzcd}
    \end{displaymath}
    Assume that $\mathcal{D}_Z$ and $\mathcal{D}_U$ are weakly approximable. Suppose $G^\prime$ is a compact generator of $\mathcal{D}$.
    Then $\mathcal{D}$ is weakly approximable if, and only if, $\operatorname{Hom}(G^\prime,G^\prime[n])=0$ for $n\gg 0$.
\end{theorem}


The proof of \cite[Theorem 1.2]{DeDeyn/Lank/ManaliRahul/Peng:2025} does not rely on weak approximability of $D_{\operatorname{qc},Z}$ for algebraic stacks. 
As an application of \Cref{thm:glueing_weak_approx}, we establish weak approximability for algebraic spaces (see \Cref{rmk:stacks_version} for algebraic stacks). 

\begin{corollary}
    \label{cor:weak_approx_spaces}
    Let $X$ be a quasi-compact quasi-separated algebraic space.
    Suppose $S\subseteq |X|$ is a closed subset with quasi-compact complement.
    Then $D_{\operatorname{qc},S}(X)$ is weakly approximable. 
    In particular, $D_{\operatorname{qc}}(X)$ is weakly approximable.
    If $X$ is Noetherian, then $D_{\operatorname{qc},S}(X)$ is coherent.
\end{corollary}

\Cref{thm:glueing_weak_approx} is the analog for weak approximability of \cite[Theorem 5.1]{Burke/Neeman/Pauwels:2023} by Burke--Neeman--Pauwels.
Loc.\ cit.\ shows that $\mathcal{D}$ is approximable if $\mathcal{D}_Z$ and $\mathcal{D}_U$ are approximable and $\operatorname{Hom}(G^\prime,G^\prime[n])=0$ for $n\gg 0$.
The `only if' direction of \Cref{thm:glueing_weak_approx} is formal (cf.\ \cite[Remark 3]{Burke/Neeman/Pauwels:2023}).
Thus, the work lies in the `if' direction.

The proof in \cite{Burke/Neeman/Pauwels:2023} lifts approximations from $\mathcal{D}_Z$ to $\mathcal{D}$.
This is done by induction on the number of cones needed to build them \cite[Lemmas 5.2 to 5.5]{Burke/Neeman/Pauwels:2023}.
At each step, the bound on the lift is worse than on its target.
Hence, the constants grow with the number of cones.
Approximability of $\mathcal{D}_Z$ bounds this number.

No such bound need exist if $\mathcal{D}_Z$ is only weakly approximable.
Instead, we lift a single shift of a compact generator so that it lies in the same shifted aisle as its target (e.g.\ $\mathcal{D}^{\leq K}$).
See \Cref{lem:onecell}.
Consequently, the cone of a lift satisfies the same bound, which gives control on the desired objects.
See \Cref{prop:closure}.

A closure argument of this type also appears in unpublished work of Pauwels. 
See \Cref{rmk:weak_approx_spaces}.
Here the constants are explicit: if $A$ works for $\mathcal{D}_Z$, $\mathcal{D}_U$ and the vanishing $\operatorname{Hom}(G,\mathcal{D}^{\leq -A})=0$ for the compact generator $G$ of \Cref{lem:normalize}\eqref{lem:normalize2}, then $3A$ works for $\mathcal{D}$.

We discuss \Cref{cor:weak_approx_spaces}.
In general for schemes, $D_{\operatorname{qc},Z}$ need not be approximable \cite[Remark 8.1]{Neeman:2024}.
Hence, \cite[Theorem 5.1]{Burke/Neeman/Pauwels:2023} does not apply, and the weak analog is necessary.
The proof of \Cref{cor:weak_approx_spaces} combines Neeman's result for schemes \cite[Theorem 3.2(iv)]{Neeman:2024} with the induction principle for algebraic spaces \cite[\href{https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09IT}{Tag 09IT}]{StacksProject}.

