Lastly, Figure~\ref{fig:Tuning} shows the smallest observed interval length $t_{\min,\text{obs}}$ in dependence of the tuning parameter $M$. While an increase in the tuning parameter $M$ causes an increase in the smallest observed interval length $t_{\min,\text{obs}}$ in this example, the theoretical bound derived in Theorem~\ref{Thm:event} is generally non-monotone in $\tilde{M}$.

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we have proposed an event-triggered zero-order hold implementation of funnel control for output reference tracking of unknown nonlinear systems by incorporating a dynamic auxiliary variable adjusting the reference trajectory. We have used the lower bound on the lengths of the sampling intervals to exclude Zeno behavior, and as a consequence, shown the same closed-loop guarantees for sufficiently fine predefined sampling schedules. Future work may address the extension to systems of arbitrary relative degree or adaptive tuning strategies for the design parameters.

