\title{Convexity, the spectrum of free Laplacians and geometric consequences: a free KLS bound}
\author{Charles-Philippe Diez$^{\dagger}$}
\thanks{$^{\dagger}$ Technion -- Israel Institute of Technology, Faculty of Mathematics, Technion City, Haifa 3200003, Israel. \textbf{author@example.org}.}


\begin{abstract}
We establish a compactness theorem for the resolvent of free
Laplacians. More precisely, let $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ be a
self-adjoint tuple admitting Lipschitz conjugate variables
in the sense of Dabrowski (2014). Under the non-commutative curvature--dimension condition $CD(c,\infty)$ with $c>0$, introduced in our previous work (2026), we prove that the free Laplacian $\Delta:=\partial^*\bar\partial$
is proper, that is, has compact resolvent. Our argument is non-perturbative and uses a spectral variant of the free Bochner method based on Dabrowski's almost-commutation formulas.

A further stochastic argument gives a normal unital trace-preserving embedding of $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ into $L(\mathbb F_2)$.
Together, the compactness and embedding theorems gives several operator-algebraic consequences, including the Haagerup approximation property, the CMAP, and strong solidity. We also obtain proper proximality and bi-exactness.

We finally obtain several geometric consequences. We prove a free analogue of Klartag's improved log--Lichnerowicz inequality (2023), together with rigidity in the equality case.
More surprisingly, under $CD(0,\infty)$, we prove a dimension-free Kannan--Lov\'asz--Simonovits (KLS) bound in the free setting. As a consequence, we obtain a free thin-shell estimate for isotropic tuples. We conclude with further questions on spectral comparison with the canonical semicircular model.
\end{abstract}


\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The classical compactness problem and its free counterpart}

The spectral theory of diffusion generators is a central theme in analysis, probability and geometry. One wants first to understand when the spectrum becomes discrete, since this analytic property already expresses strong confinement and provides a spectral decomposition of the underlying $L^2$-space. We recall the classical picture which serves as the guiding analogy for our work. Let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $\R^n$ with a smooth positive density $\rho=e^{-V}$. The weighted Laplace operator associated with $\mu$ is defined, initially on $C_c^\infty(\R^n)$, by
\[
\mathcal L_V f
=
\Delta f+\nabla(\log\rho)\cdot\nabla f
=
\Delta f-\nabla V\cdot\nabla f,
\]
and satisfies the integration-by-parts identity
\(
\int (\mathcal L_Vf)g\,d\mu=-\int\langle\nabla f,\nabla g\rangle\,d\mu.
\)
Thus $-\mathcal L_V$ is the positive Langevin generator associated with the diffusion $dX_t=-\nabla V(X_t)+\sqrt{2}dB_t$, where $(B_t)_{t\geq 0}$ is an $n$-dimensional Brownian motion. It defines a positive self-adjoint operator on $L^2(\mu)$. Smoothness and positivity of the density give the natural self-adjoint realization and identify the constants as its kernel, but they do not by themselves imply discreteness of the spectrum.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
The compactness problem is encoded by the following fundamental equivalence:
\[
(\alpha-\mathcal L_V)^{-1}\ \text{compact}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{$-\mathcal L_V$ has discrete spectrum},
\]
for $\alpha>0$ (see~\cite{Davies,ReedSimonIV} for details). Equivalently, $-\mathcal L_V$ admits a complete orthonormal basis of eigenfunctions, every eigenvalue has finite multiplicity, and the eigenvalues tend to infinity. This is of course considerably stronger than the existence of a spectral gap, which only isolates the constants from the rest of the spectrum.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
Classically, compactness of the resolvent is intimately related to confinement at infinity. Indeed, it is well known that $-\mathcal L_V$ is unitarily equivalent to the Schr\"odinger operator (see Klartag-Putterman~\cite[Remark~2.7]{KlartagPutterman})
\[
-\Delta+\frac14\abs{\nabla V}^2-\frac12\Delta V.
\]
In particular, when $\rho$ is $\mathcal C^2$ and $ {\Delta (\sqrt{\rho})}/{\sqrt{\rho}}$ tends to infinity
at infinity, the resolvent is compact (we refer to Bakry--Gentil--Ledoux~\cite[Corollary~4.10.9]{Ledoux}). In the log-concave case, one has another natural sufficient condition
\(
V(x)/\abs{x}\rightarrow+\infty
\)
as $\abs{x}\to\infty$, as obtained by Klartag--Putterman~\cite[Appendix~A]{KlartagPutterman}. Their very elegant proof in the regular log-concave case, for rapidly decreasing log-concave densities, highlights this ``confinement'' property, which in fact admits an analytic formulation through an exterior coercivity estimate: for every $a>0$, there exists a sufficiently large ball such that
\[
\int_{\R^n}\abs{\nabla f}^2\,d\mu
\geq
a\int_{\R^n}f^2\,d\mu
\]
for every function $f$ supported outside this ball. Thus, very far from the center of the measure, the energy necessarily becomes arbitrarily large. Under the regularity assumptions of~\cite[Section~2]{Klartaglog}, the eigenfunctions are moreover \emph{\(\mu\)-tempered}: they and all their partial derivatives of arbitrary order have subexponential decay relative to $\rho$~\cite[Lemma~2.2]{Klartaglog}. Here decay relative to $\rho$ means that $\sqrt\rho\,f$ decays exponentially (using again the unitary equivalence with Schr\"odinger operators, see~\cite[Theorem~XIII.70]{ReedSimonIV}).

\par\vspace{0.5em}
Let us emphasize that this classical picture separates several properties and functional inequalities which differ in importance. A Poincar\'e inequality only gives a spectral gap. A logarithmic Sobolev inequality is equivalent, in the diffusion setting, to hypercontractivity, following the seminal work of Gross~\cite{gross}. Stronger Sobolev or Nash-type estimates may imply ultracontractivity and, for a finite invariant measure, compactness or trace-class properties of the heat semigroup (see~\cite{Ledoux,Davies} for more details). None of these implications can simply be reversed. In particular, compact resolvent alone gives neither hypercontractivity nor ultracontractivity. The theory explained above extends readily to the more general setting of weighted Riemannian manifolds $(M^n,g,\mu)$, where $(M^n,g)$ is a complete smooth Riemannian manifold and $\mu=e^{-V}d\mathrm{Vol}_g$, with completeness ensuring, for example, that the associated Laplacian $\Delta_{g,\mu}$ is essentially self-adjoint on an appropriate domain containing the space of compactly supported smooth functions (see Bakry--Gentil--Ledoux~\cite[Proposition~3.2.1]{Ledoux}). We recall, for example, that on
positively curved weighted manifolds, we always have discrete spectrum, since they satisfy a log-Sobolev inequality by the Bakry--\'Emery criterion (see Bakry--\'Emery's seminal contribution for further details~\cite{Bakry}) and such a functional inequality is known to imply discreteness; we refer to~\cite{Yang,Cianchi} for precise statements.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
Our purpose in this paper is to investigate the corresponding question for the \emph{free Laplacian} in divergence form associated to closable free difference quotients. Let $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ be a finite self-adjoint tuple in a tracial $W^*$--probability space, let $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$, and denote by $\bar\partial=(\bar\partial_1,\ldots,\bar\partial_n)$ the closed free difference quotient. The natural positive self-adjoint operator is then
\[
\Delta:=\partial^*\bar\partial
=
\sum_{j=1}^n\partial_j^*\bar\partial_j.
\] This operator is the generator of a completely Dirichlet form on $L^2(M)$. In perfect analogy with the preceding classical picture, compactness of $(\alpha+\Delta)^{-1}$ for some (equivalently, every) $\alpha>0$ is equivalent to compactness of the free Sobolev embedding
\(
\Dom(\bar\partial)=\Dom(\Delta^{1/2})\hookrightarrow L^2(M),
\)
when the form domain is equipped with its graph norm. It is also equivalent to the fact that the spectrum of $\Delta$ consists only of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity tending to infinity. The problem is therefore to understand whether the free differential calculus itself contains enough confinement to force this compactness. As we will see in the sequel, the required confinement is naturally provided by a convexity condition.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
For a finite semicircular family, everything is particularly simple and explicit: $\Delta$ is the number operator on the full Fock space (see Biane--Speicher~\cite{BS}). Its eigenvalue $k$ has multiplicity $n^k$, and hence its resolvent is compact. In the infinite-dimensional setting the situation is already different, since the eigenspaces become infinite-dimensional: the Wigner chaoses are the orthogonal eigenspaces (parallel to the classical world of Wiener chaoses). Beyond the finite semicircular model, however, very little seems to be known about the spectrum of the ordinary free Laplacian. In our previous work on rigidity for the free Poincar\'e inequality~\cite{DiezObata}, compactness of the resolvent appeared precisely as the missing spectral hypothesis needed to complete the picture of our free Obata theorem. More recently, Jekel and Basa Landry~\cite{JekelLandry} emphasized that compact resolvent is not known in general even under strong analytic regularity assumptions such as finite free Fisher information or Lipschitz conjugate variables. This question can in fact be traced back to the 2006 AIM workshop on Free Analysis~\cite{Aim}, where compactness of the heat semigroup associated with the free Laplacian was asked about for limiting non-commutative laws arising from random matrix models, already in the simplest analytic perturbations of the quadratic model. The difficulty lies in the passage from finite-dimensional matrix models to the limiting free Laplacian, which is far from immediate.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
There is also a distinction that has to be made between our setting and the $q$--Gaussian case equipped with its $q$--Gaussian number operator (we do not know whether such tuples generally belong to our $CD$ class for a large range of $q$). For a finite $q$--Gaussian family (these are deformations of the free group factors) introduced by Bo\.{z}ejko, K\"ummerer and Speicher~\cite{BSK97}, $-1<q<1$, the $q$--Ornstein--Uhlenbeck operator
\(
D_q=\sum_{k\geq0}kP_k
\)
on the $q$--Fock space, where $P_k$ denotes the projection onto the $k$--th homogeneous chaos, has compact resolvent and its heat semigroup is ultracontractive by the important result of Bo\.{z}ejko~\cite{Bozejko}. At $q=0$, $D_q$ agrees with the ordinary free Ornstein--Uhlenbeck operator, which is precisely the free Laplacian associated with a semicircular family. For $q\neq0$, however, the two operators are well known to be different (this is even clearer in the infinite-dimensional setting, as highlighted in~\cite[Definition~20]{Diez2}, where the underlying derivation is not even Hilbert-Schmidt for any range of $q$; this creates, for example, numerous complications in building a robust $q$-version of the Malliavin calculus). Therefore, the spectral properties of the number-length operator do not answer the compactness question for
\(
\sum_j\partial_j^*\bar\partial_j
\)
associated with the canonical $q$--Gaussian generators. This distinction is important and the reader should keep these results in mind when comparing our results with the literature on these semigroups.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
There are finally some subtle points which have in fact deep consequences. First, the operator $\Delta$ depends on the chosen generating tuple and on its free differential calculus, rather than only on the abstract von Neumann algebra $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$. Already a change of coordinates modifies the free difference quotients through the chain rule, and therefore also the corresponding non-commutative Jacobian and Laplacian. What can be reasonably preserved, or at least compared on the spectral side, thus requires a sufficiently regular change of variables with two-sided bounds. This explains why the analytic invertibility arising in the free monotone transport of Guionnet and Shlyakhtenko~\cite{GS2} contains considerably more information than the conclusion $W^*(X)\cong L(\mathbb F_n)$ alone: the two Dirichlet forms become comparable, therefore giving compact resolvent (and even ultracontractivity).


\subsection{Positive free curvature and the spectral Bochner argument}
We now describe the curvature assumption and the spectral mechanism behind our proof. Suppose that $X$ admits Lipschitz conjugate variables $\xi=(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_n)$ in the sense of Dabrowski~\cite[Definition~1]{Dab14}. Their non-commutative Jacobian is
\[
\mathscr J\xi=(\bar\partial_j\xi_i)_{i,j=1}^n\in M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op}).
\]
Following our previous work on Obata's theorem in free probability~\cite{DiezObata}, we say that $X$ satisfies the curvature--dimension criterion $CD(c,\infty)$ with $c\geq 0$ when
\(
\mathscr J\xi\geq c.(1\otimes 1)\otimes I_n
\)
in the $C^*$--order of $M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$. The canonical examples of such $CD$ tuples are in fact provided by free Gibbs laws. For a non-commutative polynomial potential $V$ (usually self-adjoint) and a realization of its law $\tau_V$ by a bounded self-adjoint tuple $X$, the conjugate variables are obtained by evaluating the cyclic gradient $\mathscr DV(X)$, and their Jacobian becomes exactly the non-commutative Hessian $\mathscr J\mathscr DV(X)$. Our condition is, however, much more general: it is evaluated only at the realized tuple and does not require the existence of any potential. This flexibility is one of the principal advantages of working directly with Lipschitz conjugate variables: the condition is intrinsic to the chosen non-commutative differential calculus and does not require global estimates valid in arbitrary $C^*$--algebras. The picture for free Gibbs laws and that for Lipschitz conjugate variables would, however, be much closer if Voiculescu's \emph{cyclic-gradient conjecture} were true: this conjecture asks whether conjugate variables can always be approximated in $L^2$ by cyclic gradients (see \cite[Section~0.2, Q.4]{Aim} for precise details). It is believed that the answer should be yes, at least for such Lipschitz conjugate variables, as highlighted by Mai and Speicher~\cite[Remark~1.2]{MS}.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
Our main result shows that strictly positive free curvature implies that the corresponding free Laplacian is \emph{proper}: it has compact resolvent. Thus its spectrum is discrete, every eigenvalue has finite multiplicity, and the eigenvalues can accumulate only at infinity. The curvature bound also gives the expected spectral gap: below the curvature lower bound, the only spectral vectors are in fact the constants. More importantly, the same estimate allows us to control, by induction, every bounded spectral subspace.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
A related approach has recently been developed by Chandra, Gordina and Peev~\cite{CGP}, who introduce free Bakry--\'Emery calculus for free Langevin dynamics associated
with polynomial potentials. Using non-commutative
carr\'e du champ operators and their iterates, they obtain, under their curvature and analytic assumptions, gradient
estimates and a modified logarithmic Sobolev inequality for the stationary law. Their approach and ours share in common the use of Dabrowski's almost commutation formulas.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
The proof is based on a spectral version of the free Bochner argument developed in our previous work~\cite{DiezObata}, using Dabrowski's \emph{almost-commutation formulas}~\cite{Dab14} between free difference quotients and the associated Laplacian. The key observation is that, on a positive spectral subspace below a level, the Bochner estimate controls the tensor energy of the free difference quotient gradient by the difference between this level and the curvature. Projecting the gradient onto the tensor spectral subspace below this lower level then gives an injective map. Indeed, Voiculescu's free Poincar\'e inequality~\cite{Voipub} shows that the kernel of the closed free difference quotient gradient consists of the constants. This provides a free counterpart of the confinement estimates used in the classical setting.
The second ingredient is the tensor structure of the free difference quotient. The tensor Laplacian appearing in Dabrowski's almost-commutation formula~\ref{dab} is the sum of the two positive copies of \(\Delta\) acting on each one of the tensor parts. Its low-energy spectral subspaces are therefore controlled by tensor products of lower scalar spectral subspaces. Since the tuple has only finitely many generators, the free difference quotient gradient also has only finitely many components. If we start below the curvature lower bound, we may then proceed inductively through successive energy intervals, $[kc,(k+1)c),\: k\in \mathbb Z_{\geq 0}$. This proves that every bounded spectral subspace is finite-dimensional and gives an explicit recursion for the spectral counting function (despite the estimate at this stage being very rough).
\par\vspace{0.5em}
A more precise use of the same tensor spectral calculus gives a sharper estimate. Instead of counting all pairs of spectral values in a square, we keep only those belonging to the triangle determined by the condition that their sum remains below the prescribed level. This leads to a convolution inequality for the spectral counting measure and to the Catalan recursion. We obtain in this way an exponential bound for the spectral growth, together with a trace-class estimate for the heat semigroup. The preceding bound is still not expected to be sharp. We have not,
however, been able to push the argument far enough to remove the
Catalan numbers. The semicircular case has an additional very rigid structure
which can be explained by ``slicing'' Dabrowski's almost-commutation formula using the
conditional expectation \(
\tau\otimes\mathrm{id}
\).
This leads to the exact
multiplicities \(n^k\), instead of the Catalan multiplicities appearing
in our general argument. We do not know for now how to adapt this method in the general case.
\subsection{Consequences and applications}
Under the same positive-curvature assumption, we also construct a normal unital trace-preserving embedding of
$M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ into $L(\mathbb F_2)$, using Dabrowski's
stationary construction for a free stochastic partial differential
equation~\cite{Dab14}. This argument uses only the $CD(c,\infty)$ criterion and does not rely on compactness of the resolvent. The compactness
theorem, in turn, has several consequences beyond discreteness of the spectrum, connecting the spectral properties of the free Laplacian
with the structure of the von Neumann algebra generated by the tuple.
The two approaches are complementary: compactness provides approximation maps and allows us to apply proper-derivation methods introduced by various authors~\cite{OP1,OP2,DKEP,DP},
while the embedding yields additional approximation and structural properties that we do not obtain from compactness alone.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
The first direct consequence of compactness is the \emph{Haagerup
approximation property}, introduced by Haagerup~\cite{Haag} for free
groups and developed by Choda~\cite{Choda} in the von Neumann algebra
setting. Let us recall that for a discrete group von Neumann algebra
$L(\Gamma)$, this property is equivalent to Gromov's a-T-menability
of $\Gamma$, that is, to the existence of a $1$-cocycle
$b:\Gamma\to H$ with values in a unitary representation of $\Gamma$
such that $\gamma\mapsto\|b(\gamma)\|_H$ is proper.
In our situation the proof is immediate: the resolvents of the
completely Dirichlet form are normal unital completely positive
trace-preserving maps, their $L^2$--implementations are compact,
and they converge strongly to the identity. Thus compactness of
the resolvent gives this approximation property directly.

\par\vspace{0.5em}
We also return to the stationary solutions of the free stochastic
partial differential equation constructed by Dabrowski~\cite{Dab14}.
His construction gives normal unital trace-preserving embeddings
of $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ into the free product with a free
Brownian algebra, i.e. $M*L(\mathbb F_\infty)$. Combining his stochastic energy identity with the
resolvent form of almost commutation, also used in our previous
work~\cite{DiezObata}, we show that $CD(c,\infty)$ with $c>0$ provides sufficient
integrability to pass to the limit in the mild equation and obtain a normal unital trace-preserving embedding \(
M\hookrightarrow L(\mathbb F_\infty),
\) and consequently into $L(\mathbb F_2)$.
This argument using positive curvature directly is of independent interest as it does not require using
compactness of the resolvent. In particular, $M$ is
$R^\omega$--embeddable, where $R$ denotes the hyperfinite
$\mathrm{II}_1$ factor.
The embedding then also gives the \emph{weak$^*$ complete metric
approximation property} (CMAP) and weak exactness, and another direct way to deduce the Haagerup property.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
A further consequence of compactness uses the coarse
structure of the derivation more deeply. The free difference quotient takes
values in a finite direct sum of coarse correspondences. Combining
this fact with compactness of the associated free Laplacian and
the proper-derivation method of Ozawa--Popa~\cite{OP1,OP2}, we obtain
a weakly compact-normalizer statement and, as a consequence,
\emph{solidity} of $M$. Under the weak$^*$ completely bounded
approximation property, Ozawa's theorem~\cite{OzawaWeak} upgrades
the same argument to \emph{strong solidity}, introduced in the
work of Ozawa--Popa~\cite{OP1,OP2}. This additional approximation
hypothesis is now automatic in our positive-curvature setting,
since the embedding gives CMAP. Thus $M$ is strongly solid, as
are its unital von Neumann subalgebras.
For $n\geq2$, Dabrowski's results~\cite{Dab10} show that $M$ is
a non-$\Gamma$ $\mathrm{II}_1$ factor (more precisely, it has a non-gamma set in the sense of Peterson~\cite{Peterson}: it satisfies a spectral gap, as obtained by Amrutam--Jekel--Wasilewski~\cite[Theorem~3.8]{jek3}). It follows that $M$ is prime and has
no Cartan subalgebra. Primeness was already obtained by
Dabrowski--Ioana~\cite{Dabioa}, under the Lipschitz assumption used here but without assuming curvature. The additional
conclusion here is the strong solidity statement.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
The last operator-algebraic applications combine compactness of the
free Laplacian with the work of
Ding--Kunnawalkam Elayavalli--Peterson~\cite{DKEP} and
Ding--Peterson~\cite{DP}, which extends the notions of proper
proximality and bi-exactness  from groups to von Neumann algebras
using the \emph{small-at-infinity} operator system.
Their results apply directly in our setting. Indeed, the free
difference quotient is a closable real derivation into a finite
direct sum of coarse correspondences, while compactness of the
resolvent of $\Delta$ implies that, for every $\alpha>0$, both
\(
 \rho_\alpha:=\alpha(\alpha+\Delta)^{-1}
 \) and 
 \(\zeta_\alpha:=\rho_\alpha^{1/2}
             =\alpha^{1/2}(\alpha+\Delta)^{-1/2}
\)
are compact, i.e. belong to $\mathbb K(L^2(M))$. For $n\geq2$, $M$ is a non-amenable
factor, and~\cite[Proposition~9.4 and Lemma~9.5]{DKEP} therefore
give proper proximality. Under weak exactness, compactness also
gives bi-exactness  by~\cite[Theorem~7.11]{DP}. Since weak exactness
follows from the embedding, $M$ is biexact under our positive
curvature assumption, without any additional hypothesis.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
We return in the last section, Section~\ref{Sec3}, to the geometric motivation of the paper. Compactness of the resolvent ensures that the bottom of the positive spectrum is attained, which was the missing point in our previous work on Obata's rigidity phenomenon in free probability~\cite{DiezObata}, where we had to assume that the free Poincar\'e inequality was saturated at the curvature bound. This rigidity phenomenon is well known in Gamma--calculus and was already pointed out by Ledoux~\cite[Proposition~4]{Ledoux2} and Bouyrie~\cite[Lemma~5.1]{Bouyrie} and has motivated our previous work, which we briefly recall here: under \(CD(1,\infty)\) (the normalization $1$ plays no particular role here), saturation of the free Poincar\'e inequality therefore produces an eigenfunction which, after an orthogonal change of the centered variables, leads, by our rigidity theorem~\cite[Theorem~3.15 and Corollary~3.17]{DiezObata}, to a free product decomposition with a free group factor, whose rank is given by the dimension of the first non-zero eigenspace.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
A further important consequence of the discrete spectrum is a free counterpart of Klartag's improved \emph{log--Lichnerowicz} inequality obtained in his recent work on the Kannan-Lov\'asz-Simonovits (KLS) conjecture~\cite{Klartaglog} and which therefore improves our previous log--Lichnerowicz estimate obtained in~\cite[Theorem~2.20]{DiezObata}.
The proof follows Klartag's ``preferred-direction'' mechanism (also of importance in the ``hot-spots'' conjecture in Euclidean geometry~\cite{Judge}), with the tensor spectral gap for \(\Delta^\otimes\) replacing its classical counterpart. We are also able to identify the equality case, thus providing a counterpart to Courtade and Fathi's result~\cite{courtfa}: saturation of the improved estimate implies saturation of the usual free Lichnerowicz inequality, and our free Obata theorem then shows that a semicircular factor splits off in a direction of maximal variance. These analogies naturally lead us to a free counterpart of the
Kannan--Lov\'asz--Simonovits conjecture~\cite{KLS}, whose classical
formulation was recently resolved by Bizeul, Klartag and
Lehec~\cite{bizkla} and by Song and Zhang~\cite{song}. Bizeul, Klartag and
Lehec~\cite{bizkla} used a first version of a preprint of Song and Zhang~\cite{song} which subsequently solve it themselves. The conjecture  asks in fact (in one of its formulations) whether the Poincar\'e constant of a log-concave probability measure is bounded, up to a universal constant, by the operator norm of its covariance matrix (see Klartag and Lehec~\cite{klalehec} and the survey of Lee and Vempala~\cite{lee} for precise accounts of the conjecture and its formulations). 
Quite surprisingly, we prove that, under $CD(0,\infty)$, the free Poincar\'e constant is bounded
by a universal multiple of the operator norm of the covariance
matrix, independently of the number of variables. 
The proof combines the free Bochner formula with Poisson equations
for the partial traces of the free gradient, obtained by composing the conditional expectation with the free difference quotients $(\mathrm{id}\otimes \tau)\circ \partial_i$. Co-associativity allows us
to use both tensor factors to obtain the strict improvement needed
for this comparison. As a consequence, we also obtain a free
thin-shell estimate: for a centered isotropic tuple, the variance
of the sum of squares is bounded by a universal multiple of the
number of variables.
\par\vspace{0.5em}
We finally discuss a further spectral question, motivated by the sharp spectral comparison theorem of E. Milman~\cite{MilmanSpectral} in the classical setting, which shows that all the ordered eigenvalues of a weighted Euclidean space equipped with the standard Euclidean metric \((\mathbb R^n,\lvert\cdot\rvert,\mu)\) satisfying \(CD(c,\infty)\) are bounded from below by the corresponding eigenvalues of the Gaussian model. We prove a one-dimensional free counterpart. Note that, classically, the Euclidean norm plays a fundamental role; a counterexample for a non-Euclidean metric on the plane was recently found by Dudarov--Mikulincer~\cite[Theorem~2]{Mikulin}.
\section{Preliminaries}\label{prelim}

\subsection{Free difference quotients and the associated Dirichlet form}

Throughout this paper, $(\mathcal M,\tau)$ will denote a tracial $W^*$--probability space, which means that $\mathcal M$ is a finite von Neumann algebra endowed with a faithful normal tracial state $\tau$. We consider a finite self-adjoint tuple $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_n)\in\mathcal M_{sa}^n$, and we set $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ and $M_0=\C\langle X_1,\ldots,X_n\rangle$. Whenever the free difference quotients are considered, the tuple is always assumed algebraically free.
\newline
We write $L^2(M):=L^2(M,\tau)$, with the convention $\ip{x}{y}_2:=\tau(y^*x)$ and $\norm{x}_2:=\ip{x}{x}_2^{1/2}$. We will use without further mention the same notation for the canonical inner products and norms on $L^2(M^{op})$, on $L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op})$, and on their finite Hilbert direct sums.
\newline
We continue to write 
$\tau(x) := \langle x,1\rangle_{2}$
for the vector functional associated with the cyclic vector $1$, which extends the tracial state from $\mathcal M$ to all of $L^2(\mathcal M)$ by continuity. The same applies to the tensor extension $\tau\otimes\tau$ of the trace on $L^2( M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op})$.
\newline
Finally, unless otherwise indicated, $\lVert\cdot\rVert$ denotes the operator norm on some $\mathbb B(\mathcal H)$.

\begin{definition}[Partial free difference quotients]
The \emph{partial free difference quotients} are defined as the derivations
\[
\partial_i:M_0\to L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}),
\]
which are determined on the generators by
\(
\partial_i(X_j)=\delta_{ij}(1\otimes1).
\)
\newline
We recall that the free difference quotients are \emph{real} derivations: for every $x\in M_0$,
\[
\partial_i(x^*)=(\partial_i(x))^\dagger,
\]
where $(a\otimes b)^\dagger:=b^*\otimes a^*$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{real}
Suppose that each $\partial_i$ is closable, viewed as an unbounded operator
\(
L^2(M)\to L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}),
\)
and denote by $\bar\partial_i$ its closure. The row operator $\partial=(\partial_1,\ldots,\partial_n)$ on $M_0$ is also closable; throughout the paper, $\bar\partial$ denotes its closure. Thus $M_0$ is a core for $\bar\partial$, and
\[
\Dom(\bar\partial)\subseteq\bigcap_{i=1}^n\Dom(\bar\partial_i),
\qquad
\bar\partial x=(\bar\partial_1x,\ldots,\bar\partial_nx),
\quad x\in\Dom(\bar\partial).
\]
The real property of the free difference quotients then gives, by the usual double-adjoint argument, that $\Dom(\bar\partial)$ is $^*$--stable and
\[\label{adjunc}
\bar\partial_i(x^*)=(\bar\partial_i(x))^\dagger,
\qquad x\in\Dom(\bar\partial).
\]
We refer to Dabrowski~\cite{Dab10} for the complete argument.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[{Free Fisher information, Voiculescu~\cite[Definition~6.1]{Voic}}]
We define the \emph{free Fisher information} of $(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ by
\[
\Phi^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)
:=\sum_{i=1}^n\norm{\partial_i^*(1\otimes1)}_2^2,
\]
whenever each $\partial_i^*(1\otimes1)$ exists in $L^2(M)$; otherwise, we set $\Phi^*(X)=+\infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[{Closability, Voiculescu~\cite[Lemma~3.3]{Voic}}]
Whenever $\Phi^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)<\infty$, each $\partial_i$ is closable as an unbounded operator
\[
L^2(M)\to L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}).
\]
We denote again by $\bar\partial_i$ its closure, and by $\bar\partial$ the closure of the row operator $\partial=(\partial_1,\ldots,\partial_n)$ on $M_0$, as in Remark~\ref{real}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[{Dabrowski~\cite[Definition~1]{Dab14}}]\label{lip}
Let $M=(W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n),\tau)$ be a tracial von Neumann algebra. We say that $M$ satisfies the \emph{Lipschitz conjugate variable condition} whenever:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the partial free difference quotients $\partial_i$ are densely defined and closable,
\item the conjugate variables $\xi_i:=\partial_i^*(1\otimes1)$ exist in $L^2(M)$ for every $i=1,\ldots,n$, belong to $\Dom(\bar\partial)$, and satisfy
\[
\bar\partial_j\partial_i^*(1\otimes1)
\in M\bar\otimes M^{op}
\subset L^2(M\bar\otimes M^{op})
\cong L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op})
\cong L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M)
\]
for every $i,j=1,\ldots,n$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

We now recall a useful consequence of the Lipschitz conjugate variable condition, first observed by Dabrowski in~\cite[Sketch of the proof of Corollary~25~(a')]{Dab14} and also used in our previous work~\cite{DiezObata}: the first- and second-order conjugate variables are in fact bounded. This fact will be used several times below.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop5}
Assume that $M=(W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n),\tau)$ satisfies the Lipschitz conjugate variable condition. We then have:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\xi_i:=\partial_i^*(1\otimes1)\in M$ for every $i$,
\item $\xi_i^{(2)}:=\partial_i^*(1\otimes\partial_i^*(1\otimes1))=\partial_i^*(1\otimes\xi_i)=\partial_i^*(\xi_i\otimes1)\in M$ for every $i$,
\item $(\partial_i\otimes\mathrm{id})\circ\partial_j:M_0=\A\to L^2(M)^{\bar\otimes3}$ is densely defined and closable for every $i,j=1,\ldots,n$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

The closure of the free difference quotient gives rise to a completely Dirichlet form. We now collect the properties of its generator, as well as the resolvent approximations.

\begin{proposition}[Cipriani--Sauvageot~\cite{Sauvage,CiprianiSauvageot2003}, Peterson~\cite{Peterson}, Dabrowski~\cite{Dab10}]\label{resolvent}
Let $M=W^*(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ be the tracial von Neumann algebra generated by self-adjoint variables $(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ in a tracial $W^*$--probability space $(\mathcal M,\tau)$, and suppose that the partial free difference quotients are closable. Then the following assertions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item The operator
\(
\Delta:=\partial^*\bar\partial,
\)
where $\bar\partial=(\bar\partial_1,\ldots,\bar\partial_n)$, is the generator of a completely Dirichlet form on $L^2(M)$; see~\cite{Sauvage}. In particular, all its matrix amplifications are again Dirichlet.

\item The resolvent maps
\(
\rho_\alpha:=\alpha(\alpha+\Delta)^{-1},
\)
$\alpha>0$, are normal, unital, tracial $(\tau\circ\rho_\alpha=\tau)$, completely positive, and contractive on both $L^2(M)$ and $M$; see, for instance,~\cite{Ma}. We also have
\[
\norm{x-\rho_\alpha(x)}\leq2\norm{x},
\qquad
\norm{x-\rho_\alpha(x)}_2\to0
\quad(\alpha\to\infty)
\]
for every $x\in M$, as follows from~\cite[Proposition~2.5]{CiprianiSauvageot2003}.

\item The semigroup $\varphi_t=e^{-t\Delta}$ is strongly continuous with generator $-\Delta$, and
\[
\rho_\alpha
=\alpha\int_0^\infty e^{-\alpha t}\varphi_t\,dt,
\]
where the integral is understood with respect to the strong operator topology on $L^2(M)$.

\item The square-root resolvent
\[
\zeta_\alpha:=\rho_\alpha^{1/2}
=\pi^{-1}\int_0^\infty\frac{t^{-1/2}}{1+t}\,
\rho_{\alpha(1+t)/t}\,dt
\]
satisfies
\[
\Ran(\rho_\alpha)=\Dom(\Delta)\subset\Dom(\bar\partial),
\qquad
\Ran(\zeta_\alpha)=\Dom(\Delta^{1/2})=\Dom(\bar\partial),
\]
and therefore $\bar\partial\circ\zeta_\alpha$ is bounded (see~\cite{Peterson}).
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{flushleft}
We will also use throughout the paper Voiculescu's fundamental free Poincar\'e inequality. Let us emphasize that it holds for every bounded self-adjoint tuple, with no assumption on its law.
\end{flushleft}

\begin{lemma}[Free Poincar\'e inequality, Voiculescu~\cite{Voipub}]\label{voipub}
Let $Y\in\Dom(\bar\partial)$ be self-adjoint or non-self-adjoint. Then there exists a constant $C>0$, which depends on the $X_i$ but not on $Y$, such that
\[
\norm{Y-\tau(Y).1}_2^2
\leq
C\sum_{j=1}^n\norm{\bar\partial_jY}_2^2.
\]
Moreover, if $C_P:=C_P(X)$ denotes the optimal constant, called the \emph{free Poincar\'e constant}, then
\[
C_P\leq4n\max_{i=1,\ldots,n}\norm{X_i}^2.
\]
\end{lemma}

A direct consequence of the free Poincar\'e inequality is that it identifies the kernel of the closed free difference quotient, and therefore also the zero eigenspace of the free Laplacian.

\begin{proposition}\label{kerprop}
Suppose that the partial free difference quotients are closable. We have
\begin{equation}\label{kernel}
\Ker(\Delta)=\Ker(\bar\partial)=\mathbb C\,1.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

Let $ O:L^2(M)\to L^2(M^{op})$ be the canonical unitary $ O(x)=x^{op}$ coming from traciality. In the sequel, and by a slight abuse of notation, the same symbol $\Delta$ denotes $ O\Delta O^{-1}$ on $L^2(M^{op})$, and $E(B)$ denotes the corresponding transported spectral projection on $L^2(M^{op})$. We use the same convention below when dealing with Dabrowski's almost-commutation formulas~\ref{dab} (see~\cite{Dab14,DiezObata} for more details).

\begin{proposition}[Tensor spectral calculus]\label{tensor}
The positive self-adjoint operators $\Delta\otimes \mathrm{id}$ and $\mathrm{id}\otimes\Delta$ on $L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op})$ strongly commute. If $E(\cdot)$ denotes the spectral measure of $\Delta$, with our preceding convention on the $L^2(M^{op})$ leg, their joint spectral measure $\mathbb E$ is characterized by
\[
\mathbb E(B\times C)=E(B)\otimes E(C),
\qquad
B,C\subset[0,\infty)\ \text{Borel}.
\]
The operator
\begin{equation}\label{delta2}
\Delta^\otimes
:=\int_{[0,\infty)^2}(\lambda+\mu)\,d\mathbb E(\lambda,\mu)
\end{equation}
is the closure of $\Delta\otimes \mathrm{id}+\mathrm{id}\otimes\Delta$ on $\Dom(\Delta)\otimes\Dom(\Delta)$ (where $\otimes$ denotes here the algebraic tensor product). Moreover, for every $s\geq0$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{tensorproj}
\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta^\otimes)
\leq
\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta)\otimes\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta).
\end{equation}
Consequently, if $N_\Delta(s):=\rank\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta)<\infty$, we get
\[
\rank\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta^\otimes)
\leq
N_\Delta(s)^2.
\]
Finally,
\begin{equation}\label{tensorker}
\Ker(\Delta^\otimes)
=\Ker(\Delta)\bar\otimes\Ker(\Delta)
=\mathbb C.(1\otimes 1).
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The spectral projections of $\Delta\otimes \mathrm{id}$ and $\mathrm{id}\otimes\Delta$ are $E(B)\otimes \mathrm{id}$ and $\mathrm{id}\otimes E(C)$, and they therefore commute strongly. The joint spectral theorem gives the preceding identity together with the operator in~\eqref{delta2}. It remains first to verify the assertion concerning the core. For $n\geq 0$, put 
\(
Q_n:=E([0,n])\otimes E([0,n]).
\)
For every $x\in\Dom(\Delta^\otimes)$, the joint spectral calculus gives $Q_nx\to x$ in the graph norm of $\Delta^\otimes$. On $\Ran(Q_n)$, this graph norm is equivalent to the Hilbert-space norm, and 
\[
E([0,n])L^2(M)\otimes E([0,n])L^2(M^{op})
\]
is dense in $\Ran(Q_n)$ and contained in $\Dom(\Delta)\otimes\Dom(\Delta)$. Hence, the algebraic tensor product is indeed a core.
\newline
We now verify the projection estimate and set
\[
T_s:=\{(\lambda,\mu)\in[0,\infty)^2:\lambda+\mu\leq s\}.
\]
Then
\[
\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta^\otimes)=\mathbb E(T_s),
\qquad
\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta)\otimes\mathds1_{[0,s]}(\Delta)
=\mathbb E([0,s]^2).
\]
Since $0\leq \lambda+\mu\leq s$ implies $0\leq \lambda,\mu\leq s$, we have the immediate inclusion $T_s\subset[0,s]^2$, and the monotonicity of projection-valued measures yields~\eqref{tensorproj}. The range of the projection in the right-hand side of~\eqref{tensorproj} has dimension $N_\Delta(s)^2$, proving the desired rank estimate. Finally, since $\lambda,\mu\geq0$, the equality $\lambda+\mu=0$ is equivalent to $\lambda=\mu=0$, and~\eqref{tensorker} follows immediately from~\eqref{kernel}. This concludes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}\label{rightaction}
Let $(M,\tau)$ be a tracial $W^*$--probability space. We first define on the algebraic tensor product $L^2(M)\otimes L^2(M^{op})$, for $x\otimes y\in M\otimes M^{op}$ and $a\otimes b\in L^2(M)\otimes L^2(M^{op})$,
\[
(a\otimes b)\sharp(x\otimes y):=ax\otimes by.
\]
Here the product on the second tensor leg is taken in $M^{op}$. By bilinearity and continuity, this extends to a bounded right action of $M\bar\otimes M^{op}$ on $L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op})$.

For $n\in\N$ and $T=(T_{ij})_{i,j=1}^n\in M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$, we then define the \emph{right-leg} multiplication
\[
\mathcal R_T:
(L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}
\to
(L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}
\]
by
\[
(\mathcal R_T\eta)_i:=\sum_{j=1}^n\eta_j\sharp T_{ji},
\qquad i=1,\ldots,n,
\]
for $\eta=(\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_n)\in(L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{flushleft}
We now recall the useful representation properties of $\mathcal R$ obtained in our previous work~\cite[Proposition~2.18]{DiezObata}.
\end{flushleft}

\begin{proposition}\label{rightprop}
For each $n\geq1$ and $T,S\in M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$, the operators introduced in Definition~\ref{rightaction} enjoy the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \emph{Linearity:} $\mathcal R_{T+S}=\mathcal R_T+\mathcal R_S$.
  \item \emph{Boundedness:}
  \[
  \norm{\mathcal R_T}\leq \norm{T}_{M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})}.
  \]
  Hence
  \[
  \mathcal R_T\in B\!\left((L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}\right)
  \cong M_n\!\left(B(L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))\right).
  \]
  \item \emph{$^*$--structure:} $\mathcal R_{T^*}=(\mathcal R_T)^*$, where $(T^*)_{ij}=T_{ji}^*$ for the usual $^*$--involution on $M\bar\otimes M^{op}$.


\item $\mathcal R_{(1\otimes 1)\otimes I_n}=\mathrm{id}_{(L^2(M){\bar\otimes}\,L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}}$.
  \item \emph{Multiplication rule:} Writing $TS$ for the matrix product formed using the multiplication $\sharp$ in $M\bar\otimes M^{op}$, we have


\[
  \mathcal R_S\circ\mathcal R_T=\mathcal R_{T S}=\mathcal R_{T\sharp S}
  \qquad\text{for all }T,S\in M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op}).
  \]
In particular, whenever $T$ is invertible in $M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$, the operator $\mathcal R_T$ is invertible and $(\mathcal R_T)^{-1}=\mathcal R_{T^{-1}}$.


    \item \emph{Positivity:}
  if $T\ge 0$ in $M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$ (resp.\ $T>0$, i.e.\ $T\ge c.(1\otimes 1)\otimes I_n$ for some $c>0$), then 
  $\mathcal R_T\ge 0$ (resp.\ $\mathcal R_T\ge c\,\mathrm{id}$) as an operator on 
  $(L^2(M)\bar\otimes L^2(M^{op}))^{\oplus n}$.
\end{enumerate}
Thus, $T\mapsto\mathcal R_T$ is a $*$--anti-representation of $M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})$, or equivalently a $*$--representation of the opposite algebra $M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})^{op}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{flushleft}
We also need the following important symmetry property for the free Jacobian of the conjugate variables.
\end{flushleft}
\begin{proposition}\label{proplip}
Under Definition~\ref{lip}, the matrix
\[
\mathscr J\xi
=(\bar\partial_j\xi_i)_{i,j=1}^n
\in M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op})
\]
is self-adjoint.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
This was proved in~\cite[Lemma~2.19]{DiezObata}. We nevertheless recall the short argument for the convenience of the reader.
\newline
We denote by $\dagger$ the isometric Hilbert--Schmidt involution on $M\otimes M^{op}$, given on simple tensors by
\(
(a\otimes b^{op})^\dagger=b^*\otimes(a^*)^{op},
\)
and let
\(
\sigma(a\otimes b^{op})=b\otimes a^{op}
\)
denote the flip. The real property of the free difference quotients first gives
\(
(\bar\partial_j\xi_i)^\dagger=\bar\partial_j\xi_i,
\)
whereas the Schwarz symmetry of the conjugate system gives
\(
\bar\partial_j\xi_i=\sigma(\bar\partial_i\xi_j)
\). Since $\sigma$ is an involutive $*$--anti-automorphism, we obtain


\[
      (\bar\partial_j\xi_i)^\ast=\sigma\big((\bar\partial_j\xi_i)^{\dagger}\big)
      =\sigma(\bar\partial_j\xi_i)=\bar\partial_i\xi_j.
    \]
This proves the self-adjointness of $\mathscr J\xi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[{Diez~\cite[Theorem~2.20]{DiezObata}}]\label{CD}
We say that $X$ satisfies the curvature--dimension criterion $CD(c,\infty)$ whenever it has Lipschitz conjugate variables and
\[
\mathscr J\xi
\geq
c.(1\otimes 1)\otimes I_n
\qquad
\text{in }M_n(M\bar\otimes M^{op}).
\]
\end{definition}


\begin{lemma}[{Dabrowski~\cite[Lemma~19]{Dab14}}]\label{dab}
Under the assumptions in Definition~\ref{lip}, we have,%Assume Assumption 1.
\begin{enumerate}
\item
Set $\Delta_j:=\partial_j^*\bar\partial_j$. For any $x\in M_0$, we have 
$x\in \Dom(\Delta_j)$, %and $\lvert\lvert\delta_{j}^{*}\delta_{j}^{(Q)}(x)-\Delta_j(x)\lvert\lvert_2\to 0$.
$\partial_i(x)\in \Dom(\Delta_j\otimes1+1\otimes \Delta_j)$, $x\in \Dom(\Delta^{3/2})$ and:
 \begin{align*} 
&{\partial}_{i}\Delta_{j}(x)= (1 \otimes \Delta_j+\Delta_{j}\otimes 1){\partial}_i(x)%+\sum_{k=N+1}^{2N} \tilde{\delta}_k(x)\#((1\o\delta_{j}^*)C_{i+N,j}^k-(\delta_{j}^*\o 1)C_{j,i+N}^k)
+\partial_{j}(x)\#(\bar{\partial}_{i}\partial_{j}^{*}(1\otimes 1) ).
\end{align*}
\item
 If $x\in \Dom(\bar{\partial})$ (resp. $x\in \Dom(\Delta)$), then so is $\mathrm{id}\otimes\tau({\bar{\partial}_i}(x))$. 
\item
$\Dom(\Delta^{3/2})\subset \Dom(\overline{\Delta \otimes\mathrm{id}+\mathrm{id}\otimes \Delta }\circ\bar{\partial} )$ and, moreover, we have, for any $x\in \Dom(\Delta^{3/2})$, \begin{align*} 
&\bar{\partial}_{i}\Delta(x)= \Delta^{\otimes}\bar{\partial}_i(x)%+\sum_{k=N+1}^{2N} \tilde{\delta}_k(x)\#((1\o\delta_{j}^*)C_{i+N,j}^k-(\delta_{j}^*\o 1)C_{j,i+N}^k)
+\sum_{j=1}^n\bar{\partial}_{j}(x)\#(\bar{\partial}_{i}\partial_{j}^{*}(1\otimes 1) ).
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
This operator is the closure of $\Delta\otimes\mathrm{id}+\mathrm{id}\otimes\Delta$ on the core $\Dom(\Delta)\otimes\Dom(\Delta)$, where $\otimes$ denotes the algebraic tensor product. Moreover, $\Dom(\Delta^\otimes)$ is invariant under $\varphi_t\otimes\varphi_t$.
Finally, since
\(
(\mathscr J\xi)_{ji}=\bar\partial_i\xi_j,
\)
the last identity takes the following particularly useful vector form:
\[
\bar\partial\Delta(x)
=
(\Delta^\otimes\otimes I_n+\mathcal R_{\mathscr J\xi})\bar\partial(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
We recall at this point a standard spectral fact which will allow us to pass from one formulation of compactness to another. For a positive self-adjoint operator $A$, the operator $(\alpha+A)^{-1}$ is compact for some (equivalently, every) $\alpha>0$ if and only if $\mathds1_{[0,t]}(A)$ has finite rank for every $t\geq0$; this is also equivalent to compactness of the embedding of $\Dom(A^{1/2})$, endowed with the form graph norm, into the underlying Hilbert space. We will use these equivalences without further justification, and refer to Kato~\cite{Kato}.

\begin{flushleft}
For completeness, we recall that in our setting the resolvent set of $\Delta$ is defined by
\[
\rho(\Delta)
:=
\left\{
z\in\C:
(\Delta-z\,\mathrm{id}):\Dom(\Delta)\to L^2(M)
\text{ is bijective with bounded inverse}
\right\}.
\]
Since $\Delta\geq0$ is self-adjoint, we have
\(
\sigma(\Delta)\subset[0,\infty),
\)
and hence
\(
-1\in\rho(\Delta).
\)
It is thus enough to prove that the resolvent at this single point,
\(
(\Delta+\mathrm{id})^{-1},
\)
is compact. Indeed, compactness of the resolvent at one point of $\rho(\Delta)$ immediately implies compactness at every point of $\rho(\Delta)$, by combining the resolvent identity with the ideal property of compact operators $\mathbb K(L^2(M))$:
\[
(\Delta-z\:\mathrm{id})^{-1}
-
(\Delta-w\:\mathrm{id})^{-1}
=
(z-w)
(\Delta-z\:\mathrm{id})^{-1}
(\Delta-w\:\mathrm{id})^{-1},
\qquad z,w\in\rho(\Delta).
\]
\end{flushleft}

