\begin{abstract}
Let $t_k$ be the Gram points, and let $d\geq1$ be a fixed integer.  Put
\[
 \eta_d=\frac{1}{2^{d+1}-2},\qquad
 \sigma_d=1-\frac{\eta_d}{d},\qquad
 \sigma_d^*=\begin{cases}
  7/8,&d=2,\\
  \sigma_d,&d\ne2.
 \end{cases}
\]
We prove that, uniformly on compact subsets of
$\sigma_d^*<\RePart s<1$,
\[
 \frac1N\sum_{k=2}^{N}|\zeta(s+it_k^d)|^2
 \longrightarrow \zeta(2\RePart s).
\]
The underlying result is a mean-square approximation by finite Euler
products.  For the squares of Gram points, grouping equal frequency
ratios and applying the double large sieve improves the general
derivative-test boundary $11/12$ to $7/8$.  Assuming the Lindelöf
hypothesis, both
the Euler-product approximation and the second-moment formula hold on
every compact subset of $1/2<\RePart s<1$, for every fixed $d\geq1$.
As an application, we obtain a discrete universality theorem along the
same Gram-power shifts, in the same unconditional and conditional
regions.
\end{abstract}

