	\section{Introduction}

	We study the interaction of a two-dimensional incompressible viscous fluid with a purely elastic beam forming the upper boundary of a periodic channel. The beam deforms under the fluid stress, while its motion determines both the fluid domain and the velocity at the interface. Such coupling between fluid motion and structural deformation arises in blood flow through compliant vessels, flow-induced vibration in engineering, transport through flexible channels, and biological growth \cite{BGN2014,KKLTTW2018}. Its mathematical analysis combines the dissipative dynamics of the fluid with the oscillatory response of the structure on an evolving domain.

	The existence theory for fluid--structure interaction has been developed for a broad range of models. Weak solutions have been constructed for fluid--plate systems~\cite{ChambolleDesjardinsEstebanGrandmont2005,Grandmont2008,CanicMuhaTawri2026}, fluid--shell systems~\cite{LengelerRuzicka2014,MuhaCanic2013,MuhaSchwarzacher2022}, and fluids coupled to nonlinear viscoelastic bulk solids~\cite{BenesovaKampschulteSchwarzacher2024}. Compressible counterparts include~\cite{Boulakia2004,BreitSchwarzacher2018,LiuMitraNecasova2026}, while inviscid fluid-structure interaction were studied in~\cite{AlazardKukavicaTuffaha2025}. These theories admit large initial data, but their continuation generally requires that the moving geometry remain nondegenerate: contact with a fixed boundary or self-contact of the structure remains an obstruction even for weak solutions. For the fluid--beam problem, Casanova, Grandmont, and Hillairet~\cite{CasanovaGrandmontHillairet2021} constructed global weak solutions in a formulation that accommodates possible contact, without asserting a positive distance from the wall.

	Strong solutions have been obtained locally in time for beams and plates~\cite{BeiraoDaVeiga2004,Lequeurre2011,GrandmontHillairetLequeurre2019,KukavicaTuffaha2024}, shells~\cite{ChengShkoller2007,ChengShkoller2010}, and bulk elastic solids~\cite{CoutandShkoller2005,CoutandShkoller2006,KukavicaTuffaha2012,AbelsLiu2023,RaymondVanninathan2014}. Global results for sufficiently small data, such as~\cite{Lequeurre2011,IgnatovaKukavicaLasieckaTuffaha2017,Remond-TiedrezTice2019}, preserve a nondegenerate configuration through smallness of the deformation. For large data, local and maximal-in-time theories~\cite{MaityRaymondRoy2020,Breit2024,GrandmontSabbagh2025}, including recent results for perfectly elastic shells~\cite{BreitMensahSchwarzacherSu2026}, leave open the possibility of geometric degeneration.

	A fundamental question is therefore whether contact can occur in finite time. This question has been studied extensively for rigid bodies immersed in viscous incompressible fluids. Under no-slip boundary conditions, the resistance of the fluid squeezed between approaching surfaces can prevent collision. Hillairet~\cite{Hillairet2007} established such a no-collision result in two dimensions under suitable assumptions on the shapes of the body and the cavity. The outcome depends on both the boundary regularity and the coupling conditions. G\'erard-Varet and Hillairet~\cite{Gerard-VaretHillairet2010} showed that bodies with sufficiently low boundary regularity can collide even under no-slip coupling. For a sphere falling towards a flat wall, G\'erard-Varet, Hillairet, and Wang~\cite{GerardVaretHillairetWang2015} proved finite-time contact when Navier slip is imposed on both approaching surfaces. For an elastic structure, the approaching geometry is itself an unknown, and a no-contact argument must control its deformation as the fluid gap narrows.

	For the beam-fluid system, in the seminal paper ~\cite{GrandmontHillairet2016}, Grandmont and Hillairet established the first global strong no-contact result for arbitrary compatible initial data in two dimensions. Their distance estimate, combined with propagation of regularity, yields global strong solutions with initial fluid velocity in $H^1$, beam height in $H^3$, and beam velocity in $H^1$. Note that structural damping is essential to their proof, which controls the beam velocity in $L^2(0,T;H^1)$ and promotes the distance estimate. They commented in \cite[Remark~4]{GrandmontHillairet2016}:
	\begin{quote}
		\emph{To prove our distance estimate we need to control $\partial_th$ in $L^2(0,T;H_\sharp^1(0,L))$. This is the reason why we assume $\gamma>0$. One may wonder whether the fluid dissipation would be sufficient.}
	\end{quote}
	Subsequent work has developed both regularity and contact results for elastic structures. Without structural damping, Schwarzacher and Su~\cite{SchwarzacherSu2025} proved strong existence and uniqueness up to a possible contact for initial data in $H^2\times H^4\times H^2$. Their coupled acceleration estimate propagates regularity for arbitrary times as long as the beam height remains positive. Breit and Roy~\cite{BreitRoy2025} obtained a conditional no-contact criterion for compressible fluids interacting with elastic plates, with an application to the incompressible perfectly elastic beam.
	In three dimensions, Bukal, Kukavica, Li, and Muha~\cite{BKLM2026} established a conditional no-contact criterion under additional regularity assumptions on weak solutions, with or without structural damping. Their separate global existence theorem in~\cite{BKLM2026JNS} constructs weak solutions that remain uniformly separated from the bottom when the initial kinetic and bending energy satisfies a quantitative bound relative to the square of the initial mean plate height, also allowing a perfectly elastic plate. This restriction permits large absolute energy but does not cover arbitrary data at a fixed mean height.
	Recently, for compressible fluids, Trifunovi\'c~\cite{Trifunovic2026} constructed global weak solutions allowing contact and proved detachment driven by pressure and suitable forces on the plate. Tawri and Ward~\cite[Theorem~3]{TawriWard2026} proved finite-time contact for a channel with a damped elastic beam and Navier-slip conditions at the moving interface and bottom wall, under their prescribed outlet flux and pressure data.

	In this contribution, we derive a \emph{quantitative distance estimate} directly from the coupled system at the level of the mechanical energy with only the fluid dissipation, cf.~Theorem \ref{thm:separation} below. Combining with new tangential estimates, we complete the construction of global solutions with initial regularity class $H^1\times H^3\times H^1$, cf.~Theorem \ref{thm:global}. This, therefore, answers the question of Grandmont and Hillairet~\cite[Remark~4]{GrandmontHillairet2016} that \emph{the fluid dissipation does suffice for the separation}. We also derive quadratic time-weighted bounds for the higher norms, which quantify the regularity at positive times, cf.~\eqref{eq:weighted-estimate}, and lead to a spatially Lipschitz estimate for fluid velocity, cf.~\eqref{eq:velocity-lipschitz}.

	\subsection{Problem setting}
	Let $L>0$, identify the endpoints of $(0,L)$, and denote the beam height by $h(t,x)$. All unknowns are $L$-periodic in the horizontal variable $x$. The subscript $\sharp$ indicates periodicity only, and zero-mean conditions are imposed separately. Norms of periodic functions are taken over one period. For fluid function spaces, periodicity in $x$ is understood and the subscript $\sharp$ is omitted. The fluid occupies
	\begin{align}
		\Omega_h(t)=\{(x,y):x\in(0,L),\ 0<y<h(t,x)\}.\label{eq:domain}
	\end{align}
	The beam moves only vertically, with velocity $\partial_t h$. For a depiction of the geometry, see Figure \ref{fig:geometry} below. The fluid velocity $\bu=(u_1,u_2)$ and pressure $p$ satisfy
	\begin{subequations}\label{eq:model}
		\begin{align}
			\rho_f(\partial_t\bu+\bu\cdot\nabla\bu)-\Div\bsigma&=0,
			\qquad\Div\bu=0\quad\text{in }\Omega_h(t),\label{eq:fluid}\\
			\bu(t,x,0)&=0,\qquad \bu(t,x,h(t,x))= \partial_t h (t,x)\be_2,\label{eq:boundary}\\
			\rho_s\partial_t^2 h-\beta \partial_x^2 h +\alpha \partial_x^4 h 
			&=-\be_2\cdot\bsigma(t,x,h(t,x))\mathbf N_h.\label{eq:beam}
		\end{align}
	\end{subequations}
	Here $ \bsigma=2\mu D\bu-p\mathbf I $, $D\bu=\frac12(\nabla\bu+(\nabla\bu)^\top)$ and $\mathbf N_h=(- \partial_x h,1)$. All unknowns are periodic in $x$. 
	Here $\rho_f,\rho_s > 0$ are the fluid and beam mass densities, $\mu>0$ is the fluid viscosity, $\alpha>0$ is the bending coefficient, and $\beta\geq 0$ is the tension coefficient. The vector $\mathbf N_h$ is the outward normal to the fluid multiplied by the surface Jacobian. Thus the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:beam} is the vertical fluid force per unit reference length.

